ID   1.1.1.294
DE   Chlorophyll(ide) b reductase.
AN   Chl b reductase.
AN   Chlorophyll b reductase.
CA   7(1)-hydroxychlorophyllide a + NAD(P)(+) = chlorophyllide b + NAD(P)H.
CC   -!- Carries out the first step in the conversion of chlorophyll b to
CC       chlorophyll a.
CC   -!- Involved in chlorophyll degradation, which occurs during leaf
CC       senescence and it also forms part of the chlorophyll cycle, which
CC       interconverts chlorophyll a and b in response to changing light
CC       conditions.
CC   -!- While both chlorophyll a and chlorophyll b are found in higher
CC       plants, only breakdown products derived from chlorophyll a are found
CC       in the end products, i.e. non-fluorescent chlorophyll catabolites
CC       (NCCs).
CC   -!- The transition from chlorophyll b, which contains a formyl group on
CC       C7, to chlorophyll a, which contains a methyl group on C7, proceeds
CC       through the intermediate 7(1)-hydroxychlorophyll.
CC   -!- Another enzyme, which requires ferredoxin but has not been
CC       characterized fully, is required to complete the conversion.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00060;
DR   Q8LEU3, NOL_ARATH  ;  Q84ST4, NOL_ORYSJ  ;  Q93ZA0, NYC1_ARATH ;
DR   Q5N800, NYC1_ORYSJ ;
//
ID   1.1.1.295
DE   Momilactone-A synthase.
AN   Momilactone A synthase.
CA   3-beta-hydroxy-9-beta-pimara-7,15-diene-19,6-beta-olide + NAD(P)(+) =
CA   momilactone A + NAD(P)H.
CC   -!- The rice phytoalexin momilactone A is a diterpenoid secondary
CC       metabolite that is involved in the defense mechanism of the plant.
CC   -!- Momilactone A is produced in response to attack by a pathogen through
CC       the perception of elicitor signal molecules such as chitin
CC       oligosaccharide, or after exposure to UV irradiation.
CC   -!- The enzyme, which catalyzes the last step in the biosynthesis of
CC       momilactone A, can use both NAD(+) and NADP(+) but activity is higher
CC       with NAD(+).
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00060;
DR   Q7FAE1, MOMAS_ORYSJ;
//
ID   1.1.1.296
DE   Dihydrocarveol dehydrogenase.
AN   Carveol dehydrogenase.
CA   Menth-8-en-2-ol + NAD(+) = menth-8-en-2-one + NADH.
CC   -!- This enzyme from the Gram-positive bacterium Rhodococcus erythropolis
CC       DCL14 forms part of the carveol and dihydrocarveol degradation
CC       pathway.
CC   -!- Accepts all eight stereoisomers of menth-8-en-2-ol as substrate,
CC       although some isomers are converted faster than others.
CC   -!- The preferred substrates are (+)-neoisodihydrocarveol, (+)-
CC       isodihydrocarveol, (+)-dihydrocarveol and (-)-isodihydrocarveol.
//
ID   1.1.1.297
DE   Limonene-1,2-diol dehydrogenase.
CA   Menth-8-ene-1,2-diol + NAD(+) = 1-hydroxymenth-8-en-2-one + NADH.
CC   -!- While the enzyme from the Gram-positive bacterium Rhodococcus
CC       erythropolis DCL14 can use both (1S,2S,4R)- and (1R,2R,4S)-menth-8-
CC       ene-1,2-diol as substrate, activity is higher with (1S,2S,4R)-menth-
CC       8-ene-1,2-diol as substrate.
