ID   1.3.3.12
DE   L-galactonolactone oxidase.
AN   L-xylono-1,4-lactone oxidase.
CA   L-galactono-1,4-lactone + O(2) = L-ascorbate + H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- Acts on the 1,4-lactones of L-galactonic, D-altronic, L-fuconic,
CC       D-arabinic and D-threonic acids.
CC   -!- Not identical with EC 1.1.3.8 (cf. EC 1.3.2.3).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.3.24.
//
ID   1.3.3.13
DE   Albonoursin synthase.
AN   Cyclic dipeptide oxidase.
CA   Cyclo(L-leucyl-L-phenylalanyl) + 2 O(2) = albonoursin + 2 H(2)O(2).
CF   Flavoprotein.
CC   -!- The enzyme can also oxidize several other cyclo dipeptides, the best
CC       being cyclo(L-tryptophyl-L-tryptophyl) and cyclo(L-phenylalanyl-L-
CC       phenylalanyl).
DR   Q8GED9, ALBA_STRNR ;
//
ID   1.3.3.14
DE   Aclacinomycin-A oxidase.
AN   Aclacinomycin oxidoreductase.
AN   AknOx.
CA   Aclacinomycin A + O(2) = aclacinomycin Y + H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- This bifunctional enzyme is a secreted flavin-dependent enzyme that
CC       is involved in the modification of the terminal sugar residues in the
CC       biosynthesis of aclacinomycins.
CC   -!- The enzyme utilizes the same active site to catalyze the oxidation of
CC       the rhodinose moiety of aclacinomycin N to the cinerulose A moiety of
CC       aclacinomycin A (cf. EC 1.1.3.45) and the oxidation of the latter to
CC       the L-aculose moiety of aclacinomycin Y.
DR   Q0PCD7, AKNOX_STRGJ;
//
ID   1.3.3.15
DE   Coproporphyrinogen III oxidase (coproporphyrin-forming).
CA   Coproporphyrinogen III + 3 O(2) = coproporphyrin III + 3 H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- The enzyme, present in Gram-positive bacteria, participates in heme
CC       biosynthesis.
CC   -!- It can also catalyze the reaction of EC 1.3.3.4 at a lower level.
//
ID   1.3.4.1
DE   Fumarate reductase (CoM/CoB).
AN   Thiol:fumarate reductase.
CA   Fumarate + CoM + CoB = succinate + CoM-S-S-CoB.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the archaeon Methanobacterium
CC       thermoautotrophicum, is very oxygen sensitive.
CC   -!- It cannot use reduced flavins, reduced coenzyme F420, or NAD(P)H as
CC       an electron donor.
CC   -!- Distinct from EC 1.3.1.6, EC 1.3.5.1 and EC 1.3.5.4.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.3.98.2.
DR   D9PU00, TFRA_METTM ;  D9PUX5, TFRB_METTM ;
//
ID   1.3.5.1
DE   Succinate dehydrogenase (quinone).
AN   Succinate dehydrogenase (ubiquinone).
AN   Succinic dehydrogenase.
CA   Succinate + a quinone = fumarate + a quinol.
CF   FAD; Iron-sulfur.
CC   -!- The enzyme is found in the inner mitochondrial membrane in eukaryotes
CC       and the plasma membrane of many aerobic or facultative bacteria.
CC   -!- It catalyzes succinate oxidation in the citric acid cycle and
CC       transfers the electrons to quinones in the membrane, thus
CC       constituting a part of the aerobic respiratory chain (known as
CC       complex II).
CC   -!- In vivo the enzyme uses the quinone found in the organism -
CC       eukaryotic enzymes utilize ubiquinone, bacterial enzymes utilize
CC       ubiquinone or menaquinone, and archaebacterial enzymes from the
CC       Sulfolobus genus use caldariellaquinone.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 1.3.5.4.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00393;
