ID   1.4.3.6
DE   Transferred entry: 1.4.3.21 and 1.4.3.22.
//
ID   1.4.3.7
DE   D-glutamate oxidase.
AN   D-glutamic oxidase.
CA   D-glutamate + H(2)O + O(2) = 2-oxoglutarate + NH(3) + H(2)O(2).
//
ID   1.4.3.8
DE   Ethanolamine oxidase.
CA   Ethanolamine + H(2)O + O(2) = glycolaldehyde + NH(3) + H(2)O(2).
CF   Cobalt cation.
//
ID   1.4.3.9
DE   Transferred entry: 1.4.3.4.
//
ID   1.4.3.10
DE   Putrescine oxidase.
CA   Putrescine + O(2) + H(2)O = 4-aminobutanal + NH(3) + H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- 4-aminobutanal condenses non-enzymically to 1-pyrroline.
DR   P40974, PUO_KOCRO  ;
//
ID   1.4.3.11
DE   L-glutamate oxidase.
CA   L-glutamate + O(2) + H(2)O = 2-oxoglutarate + NH(3) + H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Azotobacter previously listed under this number,
CC       which did not produce H(2)O(2), was a crude cell-free extract which
CC       probably contained catalase.
//
ID   1.4.3.12
DE   Cyclohexylamine oxidase.
CA   Cyclohexylamine + O(2) + H(2)O = cyclohexanone + NH(3) + H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- Some other cyclic amines can act instead of cyclohexylamine, but not
CC       simple aliphatic and aromatic amides.
//
ID   1.4.3.13
DE   Protein-lysine 6-oxidase.
AN   Lysyl oxidase.
CA   [Protein]-L-lysine + O(2) + H(2)O = [protein]-(S)-2-amino-6-oxohexanoate
CA   + NH(3) + H(2)O(2).
CC   -!- Also acts on protein 5-hydroxylysine.
CC   -!- Catalyzes the final known enzymic step required for collagen and
CC       elastin cross-linking in the biosynthesis of normal mature
CC       extracellular matrices.
CC   -!- These reactions play an important role for the development,
CC       elasticity and extensibility of connective tissue.
CC   -!- Also active on free amines, such as cadaverine or benzylamine.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00716;
DR   F1QQC3, LOL2A_DANRE;  A1L1V4, LOL2B_DANRE;  F1RD85, LOX3A_DANRE;
DR   B8A4W9, LOX3B_DANRE;  A6H737, LOXL2_BOVIN;  E1C3U7, LOXL2_CHICK;
DR   Q9Y4K0, LOXL2_HUMAN;  P58022, LOXL2_MOUSE;  Q5RFQ6, LOXL2_PONAB;
DR   B5DF27, LOXL2_RAT  ;  Q08B63, LOXL2_XENLA;  B4F6N6, LOXL2_XENTR;
DR   P58215, LOXL3_HUMAN;  Q9Z175, LOXL3_MOUSE;  P33072, LYOX_BOVIN ;
DR   Q05063, LYOX_CHICK ;  P28300, LYOX_HUMAN ;  P28301, LYOX_MOUSE ;
DR   P45845, LYOX_PIG   ;  P16636, LYOX_RAT   ;
//
ID   1.4.3.14
DE   L-lysine oxidase.
CA   L-lysine + O(2) + H(2)O = 6-amino-2-oxohexanoate + NH(3) + H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- Also acts, more slowly, on L-ornithine, L-phenylalanine, L-arginine
CC       and L-histidine.
//
ID   1.4.3.15
DE   D-glutamate(D-aspartate) oxidase.
CA   (1) D-glutamate + H(2)O + O(2) = 2-oxoglutarate + NH(3) + H(2)O(2).
CA   (2) D-aspartate + H(2)O + O(2) = oxaloacetate + NH(3) + H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- D-glutamate and D-aspartate are oxidized at the same rate.
CC   -!- Other D-monoaminodicarboxylates, and other D- and L-amino acids,
CC       are not oxidized.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 1.4.3.7 and EC 1.4.3.1.
//
ID   1.4.3.16
DE   L-aspartate oxidase.
AN   LASPO.
CA   L-aspartate + O(2) = iminosuccinate + H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- L-aspartate oxidase catalyzes the first step in the de novo
CC       biosynthesis of NAD(+) in some bacteria.
CC   -!- O(2) can be replaced by fumarate as electron acceptor, yielding
CC       succinate.
CC   -!- The ability of the enzyme to use both O(2) and fumarate in cofactor
CC       reoxidation enables it to function under both aerobic and anaerobic
CC       conditions.
CC   -!- Iminosuccinate can either be hydrolyzed to form oxaloacetate and
CC       NH(3) or can be used by EC 2.5.1.72 in the production of quinolinate.
DR   Q8XWM7, NADB1_RALSO;  Q8XQG4, NADB2_RALSO;  Q8U8J4, NADB_AGRFC ;
DR   O66973, NADB_AQUAE ;  Q94AY1, NADB_ARATH ;  Q9KDJ5, NADB_BACHD ;
