ID   1.4.3.22
DE   Diamine oxidase.
AN   Amine oxidase.
AN   Amine oxidase (copper-containing).
AN   CAO.
AN   Copper amine oxidase.
AN   Cu-containing amine oxidase.
AN   Diamino oxhydrase.
AN   Histaminase.
AN   Histamine deaminase.
AN   Semicarbazide-sensitive amine oxidase.
AN   SSAO.
CA   Histamine + H(2)O + O(2) = (imidazol-4-yl)acetaldehyde + NH(3) +
CA   H(2)O(2).
CC   -!- A group of enzymes that oxidize diamines, such as histamine, and also
CC       some primary monoamines but have little or no activity toward
CC       secondary and tertiary amines.
CC   -!- They are copper quinoproteins (2,4,5-trihydroxyphenylalanine quinone)
CC       and, like EC 1.4.3.21 but unlike EC 1.4.3.4, they are sensitive to
CC       inhibition by carbonyl-group reagents, such as semicarbazide.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.4.3.6.
DR   Q12556, AMO1_ASPNG ;  Q59118, AMOH_ARTGO ;  P19801, AOC1_HUMAN ;
DR   Q8JZQ5, AOC1_MOUSE ;  Q9TRC7, AOC1_PIG   ;  P36633, AOC1_RAT   ;
//
ID   1.4.3.23
DE   7-chloro-L-tryptophan oxidase.
CA   7-chloro-L-tryptophan + O(2) = 2-imino-3-(7-chloroindol-3-yl)propanoate +
CA   H(2)O(2).
CC   -!- This enzyme catalyzes a step in the biosynthesis of rebeccamycin,
CC       an indolocarbazole alkaloid produced by the bacterium Lechevalieria
CC       aerocolonigenes.
CC   -!- During catalysis, the bound FAD is reoxidized at the expense of
CC       molecular oxygen, producing one molecule of hydrogen peroxide.
CC   -!- The enzyme shows significant preference for 7-chloro-L-tryptophan
CC       over L-tryptophan.
DR   Q8KHS0, REBO_NOCAE ;  Q9S3V1, VIOA_CHRVO ;
//
ID   1.4.3.24
DE   Pseudooxynicotine oxidase.
CA   4-(methylamino)-1-(pyridin-3-yl)butan-1-one + H(2)O + O(2) = 4-oxo-4-
CA   (pyridin-3-yl)butanal + methylamine + H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- This enzyme, characterized from the soil bacterium Pseudomonas sp.
CC       HZN6, is involved the nicotine degradation.
DR   H8ZPX1, PNAO_PSESP ;
//
ID   1.4.3.25
DE   L-arginine oxidase.
CA   L-arginine + H(2)O + O(2) = 5-guanidino-2-oxopentanoate + NH(3) +
CA   H(2)O(2).
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- The enzyme from cyanobacteria can also act on other basic amino acids
CC       with lower activity.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Pseudomonas sp. TPU 7192 is highly
CC       specific.
//
ID   1.4.4.1
DE   Transferred entry: 1.21.4.1.
