ID   1.8.1.4
DE   Dihydrolipoyl dehydrogenase.
AN   Dehydrolipoate dehydrogenase.
AN   Diaphorase.
AN   Dihydrolipoamide dehydrogenase.
AN   Dihydrolipoic dehydrogenase.
AN   Dihydrothioctic dehydrogenase.
AN   E3 component of alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase complexes.
AN   Glycine-cleavage system L-protein.
AN   L-protein.
AN   LDP-Glc.
AN   LDP-Val.
AN   Lipoamide dehydrogenase (NADH).
AN   Lipoamide oxidoreductase (NADH).
AN   Lipoamide reductase.
AN   Lipoamide reductase (NADH).
AN   Lipoate dehydrogenase.
AN   Lipoic acid dehydrogenase.
AN   Lipoyl dehydrogenase.
CA   Protein N(6)-(dihydrolipoyl)lysine + NAD(+) = protein N(6)-(lipoyl)lysine
CA   + NADH.
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- A component of the multienzyme 2-oxo-acid dehydrogenase complexes.
CC   -!- In the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, it binds to the core of
CC       EC 2.3.1.12 and catalyzes oxidation of its dihydrolipoyl groups.
CC   -!- It plays a similar role in the oxoglutarate and 3-methyl-2-
CC       oxobutanoate dehydrogenase complexes.
CC   -!- Another substrate is the dihydrolipoyl group in the H-protein of the
CC       glycine-cleavage system, in which it acts, together with EC 1.4.4.2
CC       and EC 2.1.2.10 to break down glycine.
CC   -!- It can also use free dihydrolipoate, dihydrolipoamide or
CC       dihydrolipoyllysine as substrate.
CC   -!- Was first shown to catalyze the oxidation of NADH by methylene blue;
CC       this activity was called diaphorase.
CC   -!- The glycine cleavage system is composed of four components that only
CC       loosely associate: the P protein (EC 1.4.4.2), the T protein
CC       (EC 2.1.2.10), the L protein (EC 1.8.1.4) and the lipoyl-bearing H
CC       protein.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.6.4.3.
