//
ID   1.10.3.10
DE   Ubiquinol oxidase (H(+)-transporting).
AN   Cytochrome bb3 oxidase.
AN   Cytochrome bd-II oxidase.
AN   Cytochrome bo oxidase.
CA   2 ubiquinol + O(2) + n H(+)(Side 1) = 2 ubiquinone + 2 H(2)O +
CA   n H(+)(Side 2).
CF   Cu cation; Heme.
CC   -!- This terminal oxidase enzyme generates proton motive force by two
CC       mechanisms: (1) transmembrane charge separation resulting from
CC       utilizing protons and electrons originating from opposite sides of
CC       the membrane to generate water, and (2) active pumping of protons
CC       across the membrane.
CC   -!- The bioenergetic efficiency (the number of charges driven across the
CC       membrane per electron used to reduce oxygen to water) depends on the
CC       enzyme; for example, for the bo(3) oxidase it is 2, while for the
CC       bd-II oxidase it is 1.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 1.10.3.14.
DR   P26458, APPB_ECOLI ;  P26459, APPC_ECOLI ;  P57543, CYOB_BUCAI ;
DR   Q8K994, CYOB_BUCAP ;  Q89AA4, CYOB_BUCBP ;  P0ABJ0, CYOB_ECO57 ;
DR   P0ABI9, CYOB_ECOL6 ;  P0ABI8, CYOB_ECOLI ;  Q9I426, CYOB_PSEAE ;
DR   Q9WWR2, CYOB_PSEPU ;
//
ID   1.10.3.11
DE   Ubiquinol oxidase (non-electrogenic).
AN   Cyanide-insensitive oxidase.
AN   Plant alternative oxidase.
AN   Ubiquinol oxidase.
CA   2 ubiquinol + O(2) = 2 ubiquinone + 2 H(2)O.
CF   Fe cation.
CC   -!- The enzyme, described from the mitochondria of plants and some fungi
CC       and protists, is an alternative terminal oxidase that is not
CC       sensitive to cyanide inhibition and does not generate a proton motive
CC       force.
CC   -!- The function of this oxidase is believed to be dissipating excess
CC       reducing power, minimizing oxidative stress, and optimizing
CC       photosynthesis in response to changing conditions.
DR   Q39219, AOX1A_ARATH;  O82807, AOX1A_ORYSJ;  O23913, AOX1B_ARATH;
DR   O82766, AOX1B_ORYSJ;  O22048, AOX1C_ARATH;  Q8W855, AOX1C_ORYSJ;
DR   Q40294, AOX1_MANIN ;  P22185, AOX1_SAUVE ;  Q07185, AOX1_SOYBN ;
DR   Q41224, AOX1_TOBAC ;  O22049, AOX2_ARATH ;  Q41266, AOX2_SOYBN ;
DR   Q40578, AOX2_TOBAC ;  Q8LEE7, AOX3_ARATH ;  O03376, AOX3_SOYBN ;
DR   Q56X52, AOX4_ARATH ;  F4P6T0, AOX_BATDJ  ;  D5JAJ1, AOX_TRAHO  ;
//
ID   1.10.3.12
DE   Menaquinol oxidase (H(+)-transporting).
AN   Cytochrome aa3-600 oxidase.
AN   Cytochrome bd oxidase.
CA   2 menaquinol + O(2) = 2 menaquinone + 2 H(2)O.
CF   Cu cation; Heme.
CC   -!- Cytochrome aa3-600, one of the principal respiratory oxidases from
CC       Bacillus subtilis, is a member of the heme-copper superfamily of
CC       oxygen reductases, and is a close homolog of the cytochrome bo3
CC       ubiquinol oxidase from Escherichia coli, but uses menaquinol instead
CC       of ubiquinol as a substrate.
CC   -!- The enzyme also pumps protons across the membrane bilayer, generating
CC       a proton motive force.
//
ID   1.10.3.13
DE   Caldariellaquinol oxidase (H(+)-transporting).
AN   Aa(3) quinol oxidase.
AN   Aa(3)-type quinol oxidase.
AN   Cytochrome aa3.
AN   Cytochrome aa3-type oxidase.
AN   Terminal quinol oxidase.
CA   2 caldariellaquinol + O(2) + n H(+)(Side 1) = 2 caldariellaquinone +
CA   2 H(2)O + n H(+)(Side 2).
CF   Cu cation.
CC   -!- The enzyme from thermophilic archaea is part of the terminal oxidase
CC       and catalyzes the reduction of O(2) to water, accompanied by the
CC       extrusion of protons across the cytoplasmic membrane.
//
ID   1.10.3.14
DE   Ubiquinol oxidase (electrogenic, non H(+)-transporting).
AN   Cytochrome bd-I oxidase.
CA   2 ubiquinol + O(2)(Side 2) + 4 H(+)(Side 2) = 2 ubiquinone + 2 H(2)O(Side
CA   2) + 4 H(+)(Side 1).
CC   -!- This terminal oxidase enzyme is unable to pump protons but generates
CC       a proton motive force by transmembrane charge separation resulting
CC       from utilizing protons and electrons originating from opposite sides
CC       of the membrane to generate water.
CC   -!- The bioenergetic efficiency (the number of charges driven across the
CC       membrane per electron used to reduce oxygen to water) is 1.
CC   -!- The bd-I oxidase from the bacterium Escherichia coli is the
CC       predominant respiratory oxygen reductase that functions under
CC       microaerophilic conditions in that organism.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 1.10.3.10.
DR   P0ABK0, CYDA_ECOL6 ;  P0ABJ9, CYDA_ECOLI ;  P0ABK1, CYDA_SHIFL ;
DR   P0ABK4, CYDB_ECO57 ;  P0ABK3, CYDB_ECOL6 ;  P0ABK2, CYDB_ECOLI ;
DR   Q2YKD6, CYDX_BRUA2 ;  P56100, CYDX_ECOLI ;
//
ID   1.10.3.15
DE   Grixazone synthase.
CA   2 3-amino-4-hydroxybenzoate + N-acetyl-L-cysteine + 2 O(2) = grixazone B
CA   + 4 H(2)O + CO(2).
CF   Cu cation.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the bacterium Streptomyces griseus,
CC       catalyzes an 8 electron oxidation.
CC   -!- Activation of the enzyme requires a copper chaperone (GriE).
CC   -!- It also acts on 3-amino-4-hydroxybenzaldehyde, giving grixazone A.
CC   -!- The second aldehyde group is presumably lost as formate.
CC   -!- The enzyme also catalyzes the reaction of EC 1.10.3.4.
DR   B1VTI5, GRIF_STRGG ;
//
ID   1.10.3.16
DE   Dihydrophenazinedicarboxylate synthase.
CA   (1) (1R,6R)-1,4,5,5a,6,9-hexahydrophenazine-1,6-dicarboxylate + O(2) =
CA   (1R,10aS)-1,4,10,10a-tetrahydrophenazine-1,6-dicarboxylate + H(2)O(2).
CA   (2) (1R,10aS)-1,4,10,10a-tetrahydrophenazine-1,6-dicarboxylate + O(2) =
CA   (5aS)-5,5a-dihydrophenazine-1,6-dicarboxylate + H(2)O(2).
CA   (3) (1R,10aS)-1,4,10,10a-tetrahydrophenazine-1-carboxylate + O(2) =
CA   (10aS)-10,10a-dihydrophenazine-1-carboxylate + H(2)O(2).
CA   (4) (1R)-1,4,5,10-tetrahydrophenazine-1-carboxylate + O(2) = (10aS)-5,10-
CA   dihydrophenazine-1-carboxylate + H(2)O(2).
CF   FMN.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the bacteria Pseudomonas fluorescens 2-79
CC       and Burkholderia lata 383, is involved in biosynthesis of the reduced
CC       forms of phenazine, phenazine-1-carboxylate, and phenazine-1,6-
CC       dicarboxylate, where it catalyzes multiple reactions.
//
ID   1.10.5.1
DE   Ribosyldihydronicotinamide dehydrogenase (quinone).
AN   N-ribosyldihydronicotinamide dehydrogenase (quinone).
AN   NQO(2).
AN   NRH:quinone oxidoreductase 2.
AN   QR2.
AN   Quinone reductase 2.
CA   1-(beta-D-ribofuranosyl)-1,4-dihydronicotinamide + a quinone = 1-(beta-D-
CA   ribofuranosyl)nicotinamide + a quinol.
CF   FAD; Zn(2+).
CC   -!- Unlike EC 1.6.5.2, this quinone reductase cannot use NADH or NADPH;
CC       instead it uses N-ribosyl- and N-alkyldihydronicotinamides.
CC   -!- Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, such as benz[a]anthracene, and the
CC       estrogens 17-beta-estradiol and diethylstilbestrol are potent
CC       inhibitors, but dicoumarol is only a very weak inhibitor.
CC   -!- This enzyme can catalyze both 2-electron and 4-electron reductions,
CC       but one-electron acceptors, such as potassium ferricyanide, cannot be
CC       reduced.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.10.99.2.
DR   P16083, NQO2_HUMAN ;  Q9JI75, NQO2_MOUSE ;  Q5RBB9, NQO2_PONAB ;
DR   Q6AY80, NQO2_RAT   ;
//
ID   1.10.9.1
DE   Plastoquinol--plastocyanin reductase.
AN   Cytochrome b6f complex.
CA   Plastoquinol + 2 oxidized plastocyanin + 2 H(+)(Side 1) = plastoquinone +
CA   2 reduced plastocyanin + 4 H(+)(Side 2).
CC   -!- Contains two b-type cytochromes, two c-type cytochromes (cn and f),
CC       and a [2Fe-2S] Rieske cluster.
CC   -!- The enzyme plays a key role in photosynthesis, transferring electrons
CC       from photosystem II (EC 1.10.3.9) to photosystem I (EC 1.97.1.12).
CC   -!- Cytochrome c-552 can act as acceptor instead of plastocyanin,
CC       but more slowly.
CC   -!- In chloroplasts, protons are translocated through the thylakoid
CC       membrane from the lumen to the stroma.
CC   -!- The mechanism occurs through the Q cycle as in EC 1.10.2.2 (complex
CC       III) and involves electron bifurcation.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.10.99.1.
