//
ID   1.11.1.22
DE   Hydroperoxy fatty acid reductase.
CA   A hydroperoxy fatty acid + NADPH = a hydroxy fatty acid + NADP(+) +
CA   H(2)O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the cyanobacterium Synechocystis
CC       PCC 6803, can reduce unsaturated fatty acid hydroperoxides and alkyl
CC       hydroperoxides.
CC   -!- The enzyme, which utilizes NADPH generated by the photosynthetic
CC       electron transfer system, protects the cells from lipid peroxidation.
DR   P74250, GPX1_SYNY3 ;  P73824, GPX2_SYNY3 ;
//
ID   1.11.1.23
DE   (S)-2-hydroxypropylphosphonic acid epoxidase.
AN   (S)-2-hydroxypropylphosphonate epoxidase.
AN   2-hydroxypropylphosphonic acid epoxidase.
AN   HPP epoxidase.
CA   (S)-2-hydroxypropylphosphonate + H(2)O(2) = (1R,2S)-1,2-
CA   epoxypropylphosphonate + 2 H(2)O.
CF   Fe cation.
CC   -!- This is the last enzyme in the biosynthetic pathway of fosfomycin,
CC       a broad-spectrum antibiotic produced by certain Streptomyces species.
CC   -!- Contains non heme iron that forms a iron(IV)-oxo (ferryl) complex
CC       with hydrogen peroxide, which functions as a proton abstractor from
CC       the substrate.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.14.19.7.
DR   Q9JN69, HPPE_PSESX ;  Q56185, HPPE_STRWE ;
//
ID   1.11.2.1
DE   Unspecific peroxygenase.
AN   Haloperoxidase-peroxygenase.
CA   RH + H(2)O(2) = ROH + H(2)O.
CF   Heme-thiolate.
CC   -!- Enzymes of this type include glycoproteins secreted by agaric
CC       basidiomycetes.
CC   -!- They catalyze the insertion of an oxygen atom from H(2)O(2) into a
CC       wide variety of substrates, including aromatic rings such as
CC       naphthalene, toluene, phenanthrene, pyrene and p-nitrophenol,
CC       recalcitrant heterocycles such as pyridine, dibenzofuran, various
CC       ethers (resulting in O-dealkylation) and alkanes such as propane,
CC       hexane and cyclohexane.
CC   -!- Reactions catalyzed include hydroxylation, epoxidation, N-oxidation,
CC       sulfooxidation, O- and N-dealkylation, bromination and one-electron
CC       oxidations.
CC   -!- They have little or no activity toward chloride.
CC   -!- Mechanistically, the catalytic cycle of unspecific (mono)-
CC       peroxygenases combines elements of the 'shunt' pathway of cytochrome
CC       P450s (a side activity that utilizes a peroxide in place of dioxygen
CC       and NAD(P)H) and the classic heme peroxidase cycle.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00394;
DR   B9W4V6, APO1_AGRAE ;
//
ID   1.11.2.2
DE   Myeloperoxidase.
AN   MPO.
AN   Verdoperoxidase.
CA   Cl(-) + H(2)O(2) + H(+) = HClO + H(2)O.
CF   Ca(2+); Heme.
CC   -!- Is present in phagosomes of neutrophils and monocytes, where the
CC       hypochlorite produced is strongly bactericidal.
CC   -!- It differs from EC 1.11.1.10 in its preference for formation of
CC       hypochlorite over the chlorination of organic substrates under
CC       physiological conditions (pH 5-8).
CC   -!- Hypochlorite in turn forms a number of antimicrobial products (Cl(2),
CC       chloramines, hydroxyl radical, singlet oxygen).
CC   -!- MPO also oxidizes bromide, iodide and thiocyanate.
CC   -!- In the absence of halides, it oxidizes phenols and has a moderate
CC       peroxygenase activity toward styrene.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00394;
DR   P05164, PERM_HUMAN ;  P11247, PERM_MOUSE ;
//
ID   1.11.2.3
DE   Plant seed peroxygenase.
AN   Plant peroxygenase.
AN   Soybean peroxygenase.
CA   RH + ROOH = ROH + ROH.
CF   Heme.
CC   -!- A heme protein with calcium binding motif (caleosin-type).
CC   -!- Enzymes of this type include membrane-bound proteins found in seeds
CC       of different plants.
CC   -!- They catalyze the direct transfer of one oxygen atom from an organic
CC       hydroperoxide, which is reduced into its corresponding alcohol to a
CC       substrate which will be oxidized.
CC   -!- Reactions catalyzed include hydroxylation, epoxidation and
CC       sulfoxidation.
CC   -!- Preferred substrate and cosubstrate are unsaturated fatty acids and
CC       fatty acid hydroperoxides, respectively.
CC   -!- Plant seed peroxygenase is involved in the synthesis of cutin.
DR   O81270, PXG1_ARATH ;  G1JSL4, PXG1_AVESA ;  Q9FLN9, PXG2_ARATH ;
DR   O22788, PXG3_ARATH ;  Q9CAB7, PXG4_ARATH ;  Q9CAB8, PXG5_ARATH ;
DR   B3H7A9, PXG7_ARATH ;  A2XVG1, PXG_ORYSI  ;  Q7FAX1, PXG_ORYSJ  ;
DR   Q9SQ57, PXG_SESIN  ;
//
ID   1.11.2.4
DE   Fatty-acid peroxygenase.
AN   CYP152A1.
AN   Fatty acid hydroxylase.
AN   P450 peroxygenase.
AN   P450SP-alpha.
CA   Fatty acid + H(2)O(2) = 3- or 2-hydroxy fatty acid + H(2)O.
CF   Heme-thiolate.
CC   -!- A cytosolic heme-thiolate protein with sequence homology to P450
CC       monooxygenases.
CC   -!- Unlike the latter, it needs neither NAD(P)H, dioxygen nor specific
CC       reductases for function.
CC   -!- Enzymes of this type are produced by bacteria (e.g. Sphingomonas
CC       paucimobilis, Bacillus subtilis).
CC   -!- Catalytic turnover rates are high compared with those of
CC       monooxygenation reactions as well as peroxide shunt reactions
CC       catalyzed by the common P450s.
CC   -!- A model substrate is myristate, but other saturated and unsaturated
CC       fatty acids are also hydroxylated.
CC   -!- Oxidizes the peroxidase substrate 3,3,5,5-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB)
CC       and peroxygenates aromatic substrates in a fatty-acid-dependent
CC       reaction.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00081;
DR   I3DZK9, CYPC_BACMM ;  O31440, CYPC_BACSU ;
//
ID   1.11.2.5
DE   3-methyl-L-tyrosine peroxygenase.
AN   3-methyl-L-tyrosine peroxidase.
AN   3-methyltyrosine peroxidase.
CA   3-methyl-L-tyrosine + H(2)O(2) = 3-hydroxy-5-methyl-L-tyrosine + H(2)O.
CF   Heme.
CC   -!- The heme-containing peroxygenase from the bacterium Streptomyces
CC       lavendulae is involved in biosynthesis of saframycin A, a potent
CC       antitumor antibiotic that belongs to the tetrahydroisoquinoline
CC       family.
DR   B0CN28, SFMD_STRLA ;
//
ID   1.12.1.1
DE   Transferred entry: 1.12.7.2.
//
ID   1.12.1.2
DE   Hydrogen dehydrogenase.
AN   Bidirectional hydrogenase.
AN   H(2):NAD(+) oxidoreductase.
AN   Hydrogenase.
AN   NAD-linked hydrogenase.
CA   H(2) + NAD(+) = H(+) + NADH.
CF   FAD or FMN; Iron-sulfur; Ni(2+).
DR   P22317, HOXF_CUPNH ;  P22658, HOXF_RHOOP ;  P22320, HOXH_CUPNH ;
DR   P22661, HOXH_RHOOP ;  P22318, HOXU_CUPNH ;  P22659, HOXU_RHOOP ;
DR   P22319, HOXY_CUPNH ;  P22660, HOXY_RHOOP ;
//
ID   1.12.1.3
DE   Hydrogen dehydrogenase (NADP(+)).
AN   NADP(+)-linked hydrogenase.
AN   NADP(+)-reducing hydrogenase.
CA   H(2) + NADP(+) = H(+) + NADPH.
CF   Fe cation; Iron-sulfur; Ni(2+).
CC   -!- The protein from the bacterium Desulfovibrio fructosovorans
CC       exclusively functions as a hydrogen dehydrogenase, while the enzyme
CC       from the archaeon Pyrococcus furiosus is part of a heterotetrameric
CC       complex where the alpha and delta subunits function as a hydrogenase
CC       while the beta and gamma subunits function as sulfur reductase
CC       (EC 1.12.98.4).
CC   -!- Different from EC 1.12.1.5.
DR   Q46505, HNDA_DESFR ;  Q46506, HNDB_DESFR ;  Q46507, HNDC_DESFR ;
DR   Q46508, HNDD_DESFR ;  E7FI44, HYD1A_PYRFU;  E7FHU4, HYD1D_PYRFU;
//
ID   1.12.1.4
DE   Hydrogenase (NAD(+), ferredoxin).
AN   Bifurcating (FeFe) hydrogenase.
CA   2 H(2) + NAD(+) + 2 oxidized ferredoxin = 5 H(+) + NADH + 2 reduced
CA   ferredoxin.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Thermotoga maritima contains a (FeFe) cluster
CC       (H-cluster) and iron-sulfur clusters.
CC   -!- It works in the direction evolving hydrogen as a means of eliminating
CC       excess reducing equivalents.
//
ID   1.12.1.5
DE   Hydrogen dehydrogenase (NAD(P)(+)).
CA   H(2) + NAD(P)(+) = H(+) + NAD(P)H.
CF   Fe cation; Iron-sulfur; Ni(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme from the archaeon Pyrococcus furiosus is part of a
CC       heterotetrameric complex where the alpha and delta subunits function
CC       as a hydrogenase while the beta and gamma subunits function as sulfur
CC       reductase (EC 1.12.98.4).
CC   -!- Different from EC 1.12.1.3.
DR   E7FHC4, HYD2A_PYRFU;  E7FHF8, HYD2D_PYRFU;
//
ID   1.12.2.1
DE   Cytochrome-c3 hydrogenase.
