ID   1.13.11.47
DE   3-hydroxy-4-oxoquinoline 2,4-dioxygenase.
AN   (1H)-3-hydroxy-4-oxoquinoline 2,4-dioxygenase.
AN   3-hydroxy-4(1H)-one, 2,4-dioxygenase.
AN   3-hydroxy-4-oxo-1,4-dihydroquinoline 2,4-dioxygenase.
AN   Quinoline-3,4-diol 2,4-dioxygenase.
CA   3-hydroxy-1H-quinolin-4-one + O(2) = N-formylanthranilate + CO.
CC   -!- Does not contain a metal center or organic cofactor.
CC   -!- Fission of two C-C bonds: 2,4-dioxygenolytic cleavage with
CC       concomitant release of carbon monoxide.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Pseudomonas putida is highly specific for this
CC       substrate.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.12.99.5 and EC 1.13.99.5.
DR   O33472, QDO_PSEPU  ;
//
ID   1.13.11.48
DE   3-hydroxy-2-methylquinolin-4-one 2,4-dioxygenase.
AN   (1H)-3-hydroxy-4-oxoquinaldine 2,4-dioxygenase.
CA   3-hydroxy-2-methyl-1H-quinolin-4-one + O(2) = N-acetylanthranilate + CO.
CC   -!- Does not contain a metal center or organic cofactor.
CC   -!- Fission of two C-C bonds: 2,4-dioxygenolytic cleavage with
CC       concomitant release of carbon monoxide.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Arthrobacter sp. can also act on 3-hydroxy-4-
CC       oxoquinoline, forming N-formylanthranilate and CO (cf.
CC       EC 1.13.11.47), but more slowly.
DR   O31266, HOD_PAENT  ;
//
ID   1.13.11.49
DE   Chlorite O(2)-lyase.
AN   Chlorite dismutase.
CA   Chloride + O(2) = chlorite.
CF   Fe cation; Heme b.
CC   -!- Reaction occurs in the reverse direction in chlorate- and
CC       perchlorate-reducing bacteria.
CC   -!- There is no activity when chlorite is replaced by hydrogen peroxide,
CC       perchlorate, chlorate or nitrite.
CC   -!- The term 'chlorite dismutase' is misleading as the reaction does not
CC       involve dismutation/disproportionation.
DR   Q47CX0, CLD_DECAR  ;  Q9F437, CLD_IDEDE  ;
//
ID   1.13.11.50
DE   Acetylacetone-cleaving enzyme.
AN   Acetylacetone dioxygenase.
AN   Diketone cleaving dioxygenase.
AN   Diketone cleaving enzyme.
CA   Pentane-2,4-dione + O(2) = acetate + 2-oxopropanal.
CF   Fe cation.
CC   -!- Forms the first step in the acetylacetone degradation pathway of
CC       Acinetobacter johnsonii.
CC   -!- While acetylacetone is by far the best substrate, heptane-3,5-dione,
CC       octane-2,4-dione, 2-acetylcyclohexanone and ethyl acetoacetate can
CC       also act as substrates.
DR   Q8GNT2, DKE1_ACIJO ;
