DE   Monocyclic monoterpene ketone monooxygenase.
AN   1-hydroxy-2-oxolimonene 1,2-monooxygenase.
AN   Dihydrocarvone 1,2-monooxygenase.
AN   MMKMO.
CA   (1) (-)-menthone + NADPH + O(2) = (4R,7S)-7-isopropyl-4-methyloxepan-2-
CA   one + NADP(+) + H(2)O.
CA   (2) Dihydrocarvone + NADPH + O(2) = 4-isopropenyl-7-methyloxepan-2-one +
CA   NADP(+) + H(2)O.
CA   (3) (Iso)-dihydrocarvone + NADPH + O(2) = 6-isopropenyl-3-methyloxepan-2-
CA   one + NADP(+) + H(2)O.
CA   (4) 1-hydroxymenth-8-en-2-one + NADPH + O(2) = 3-isopropenyl-6-
CA   oxoheptanoate + NADP(+) + H(2)O.
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- This Baeyer-Villiger monooxygenase enzyme from the Gram-positive
CC       bacterium Rhodococcus erythropolis DCL14 has wide substrate
CC       specificity, catalyzing the lactonization of a large number of
CC       monocyclic monoterpene ketones and substituted cyclohexanones.
CC   -!- Both (1R,4S)- and (1S,4R)-1-hydroxymenth-8-en-2-one are metabolized,
CC       with the lactone product spontaneously rearranging to form
CC       3-isopropenyl-6-oxoheptanoate.
DR   P82679, BVMO_RHOER ;
//
ID   1.14.13.106
DE   Epi-isozizaene 5-monooxygenase.
AN   CYP170A1.
CA   (+)-epi-isozizaene + 2 NADPH + 2 O(2) = albaflavenone + 2 NADP(+) +
CA   3 H(2)O.
CC   -!- This enzyme, from the soil-dwelling bacterium Streptomyces coelicolor
CC       A3(2), catalyzes two sequential allylic oxidation reactions.
CC   -!- The substrate epi-isozizaene, which is formed by the action of
CC       EC 4.2.3.37 is first oxidized to yield the epimeric intermediates
CC       (5R)-albaflavenol and (5S)-albaflavenol, which can be further
CC       oxidized to yield the sesquiterpenoid antibiotic albaflavenone.
DR   Q9K498, EIZFM_STRCO;
//
ID   1.14.13.107
DE   Limonene 1,2-monooxygenase.
CA   (1) (S)-limonene + NAD(P)H + O(2) = 1,2-epoxymenth-8-ene + NAD(P)+
CA   + H(2)O.
CA   (2) (R)-limonene + NAD(P)H + O(2) = 1,2-epoxymenth-8-ene + NAD(P)+
CA   + H(2)O.
CF   FAD.
CC   -!- Limonene is the most widespread terpene and is formed by more than
CC       300 plants.
CC   -!- Rhodococcus erythropolis DCL14, a Gram-positive bacterium, is able to
CC       grow on both (S)-limonene and (R)-limonene as the sole source of
CC       carbon and energy.
CC   -!- NADPH can act instead of NADH, although more slowly.
CC   -!- It has not been established if the product formed is optically pure
CC       or a mixture of two enantiomers.
DR   Q9EUT9, LIMB_RHOER ;
