ID   1.14.14.38
DE   Valine N-monooxygenase.
AN   CYP79D1.
AN   CYP79D2.
CA   L-valine + 2 O(2) + 2 [reduced NADPH--hemoprotein reductase] = (E)-2-
CA   methylpropanal oxime + 2 [oxidized NADPH--hemoprotein reductase] + CO(2)
CA   + 3 H(2)O.
CF   Heme-thiolate.
CC   -!- This enzyme catalyzes two successive N-hydroxylations of L-valine,
CC       the committed step in the biosynthesis of the cyanogenic glucoside
CC       linamarin in Manihot esculenta (cassava).
CC   -!- The product of the two hydroxylations, N,N-dihydroxy-L-valine,
CC       is labile and undergoes dehydration and decarboxylation that produce
CC       the (E) isomer of the oxime.
CC   -!- It is still not known whether the decarboxylation is spontaneous or
CC       catalyzed by the enzyme.
CC   -!- The enzyme can also accept L-isoleucine as substrate, with a lower
CC       activity.
CC   -!- It is different from EC 1.14.14.39.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.14.13.117.
DR   Q9M7B8, C79D1_MANES;  Q9M7B7, C79D2_MANES;
//
ID   1.14.14.39
DE   Isoleucine N-monooxygenase.
AN   CYP79D3.
AN   CYP79D4.
CA   L-isoleucine + 2 O(2) + 2 [reduced NADPH--hemoprotein reductase] =
CA   (1E,2S)-2-methylbutanal oxime + 2 [oxidized NADPH--hemoprotein reductase]
CA   + CO(2) + 3 H(2)O.
CF   Heme-thiolate.
CC   -!- This enzyme, found in plants, catalyzes two successive
CC       N-hydroxylations of L-isoleucine, the committed step in the
CC       biosynthesis of the cyanogenic glucoside lotaustralin.
CC   -!- The product of the two hydroxylations, N,N-dihydroxy-L-isoleucine,
CC       is labile and undergoes dehydration followed by decarboxylation,
CC       producing the oxime.
CC   -!- It is still not known whether the decarboxylation is spontaneous or
CC       catalyzed by the enzyme.
CC   -!- The enzyme can also accept L-valine, but with a lower activity.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 1.14.14.38, valine N-monooxygenase.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.4.13.117.
DR   Q6J541, C79D3_LOTJA;  Q6J540, C79D4_LOTJA;
//
ID   1.14.14.40
DE   Phenylalanine N-monooxygenase.
AN   CYP79A2.
AN   CYP79D16.
AN   Phenylalanine N-hydroxylase.
CA   L-phenylalanine + 2 [reduced NADPH--hemoprotein reductase] + 2 O(2) =
CA   (E)-phenylacetaldoxime + 2 [oxidized NADPH--hemoprotein reductase] +
CA   CO(2) + 3 H(2)O.
CF   Heme-thiolate.
CC   -!- This enzyme, found in plants, catalyzes two successive
CC       N-hydroxylations of L-phenylalanine, a committed step in the
CC       biosynthesis of benzylglucosinolate and the cyanogenic glucosides
CC       (R)-prunasin and (R)-amygdalin.
CC   -!- The product of the two hydroxylations, N,N-dihydroxy-L-phenylalanine,
CC       is labile and undergoes dehydration followed by decarboxylation,
CC       producing an oxime.
CC   -!- It is still not known whether the decarboxylation is spontaneous or
CC       catalyzed by the enzyme.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.14.13.124.
DR   Q9FLC8, C79A2_ARATH;
//
ID   1.14.14.41
DE   (E)-2-methylbutanal oxime monooxygenase.
AN   CYP71E7.
CA   (1) (E)-2-methylbutanal oxime + [reduced NADPH--hemoprotein reductase] +
CA   O(2) = 2-hydroxy-2-methylbutanenitrile + [oxidized NADPH--hemoprotein
CA   reductase] + 2 H(2)O.
CA   (2) (E)-2-methylpropanal oxime + [reduced NADPH--hemoprotein reductase] +
CA   O(2) = 2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile + [oxidized NADPH--hemoprotein
CA   reductase] + 2 H(2)O.
CC   -!- Involved in the biosynthesis of the cyanogenic glucosides
CC       lotaustralin and linamarin.
CC   -!- It catalyzes three different activities - isomerization of its
CC       substrate the (E) isomer to the (Z) isomer, dehydration,
CC       and C-hydroxylation.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.14.13.n8.
DR   Q6XQ14, C71E7_MANES;
//
ID   1.14.14.42
DE   Homomethionine N-monooxygenase.
AN   CYP79F1.
CA   An L-polyhomomethionine + 2 O(2) + 2 [reduced NADPH--hemoprotein
CA   reductase] = an omega-(methylthio)-(E)-alkanal oxime + CO(2) + 3 H(2)O +
CA   2 [oxidized NADPH--hemoprotein reductase].
CF   Heme-thiolate.
CC   -!- Involved in methionine-derived aliphatic glucosinolates biosynthesis.
CC   -!- It catalyzes two successive N-hydroxylations, which are followed by
CC       dehydration and decarboxylation.
CC   -!- CYP79F1 from Arabidopsis thaliana can metabolize homo-, di-, tri-,
