ID   1.14.99.53
DE   Lytic chitin monooxygenase.
AN   Chitin oxidohydrolase.
AN   LPMO.
CA   ((1->4)-N-acetyl-beta-D-glucosaminyl)(n+m) + reduced acceptor + O(2) =
CA   ((1->4)-N-acetyl-beta-D-glucosaminyl)(m-1)-(1->4)-2-(acetylamino)-2-
CA   deoxy-D-glucono-1,5-lactone + ((1->4)-N-acetyl-beta-D-glucosaminyl)(n) +
CA   acceptor + H(2)O.
CF   Cu(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme cleaves chitin in an oxidative manner, releasing fragments
CC       of chitin with an N-acetylamino-D-glucono-1,5-lactone at the reducing
CC       end.
CC   -!- The initially formed lactone at the reducing end of the shortend
CC       chitin chain quickly hydrolyzes spontaneously to the aldonic acid.
CC   -!- In vitro ascorbate can serve as reducing agent.
//
ID   1.14.99.54
DE   Lytic cellulose monooxygenase (C1-hydroxylating).
AN   LPMO.
AN   Lytic polysaccharide monooxygenase.
CA   ((1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl)(n+m) + reduced acceptor + O(2) = ((1->4)-beta-D-
CA   glucosyl)(m-1)-(1->4)-D-glucono-1,5-lactone + ((1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl)(n)
CA   + acceptor + H(2)O.
CF   Cu(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme, found in fungi and bacteria, cleaves cellulose in an
CC       oxidative manner.
CC   -!- The cellulose fragments that are formed contain a D-glucono-1,5-
CC       lactone residue at the reducing end, which hydrolyzes quickly and
CC       spontaneously to the aldonic acid.
CC   -!- The electrons are provided in vivo by the cytochrome b domain of
CC       EC 1.1.99.18.
CC   -!- Ascorbate can serve as the electron donor in vitro.
//
ID   1.14.99.55
DE   Lytic starch monooxygenase.
AN   LPMO.
CA   Starch + reduced acceptor + O(2) = D-glucono-1,5-lactone-terminated
CA   malto-oligosaccharides + short-chain malto-oligosaccharides + acceptor +
CA   H(2)O.
CF   Cu(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme cleaves starch in an oxidative manner.
CC   -!- It releases fragments of starch with a D-glucono-1,5-lactone at the
CC       reducing end.
CC   -!- The initially formed alpha-D-glucono-1,5-lactone at the reducing end
CC       of the shortend amylose chain quickly hydrolyzes spontaneously to the
CC       aldonic acid.
CC   -!- In vitro ascorbate has been found to be able to serve as reducing
CC       agent.
//
ID   1.14.99.56
DE   Lytic cellulose monooxygenase (C4-dehydrogenating).
CA   ((1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl)(n+m) + reduced acceptor + O(2) = 4-dehydro-beta-
CA   D-glucosyl-((1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl)(n-1) + ((1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl)(m) +
CA   acceptor + H(2)O.
CF   Cu(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme, found in fungi and bacteria, cleaves cellulose in an
CC       oxidative manner.
CC   -!- The cellulose fragments that are formed contain a 4-dehydro-D-glucose
CC       residue at the non-reducing end.
CC   -!- Some enzymes also oxidize cellulose at the C-1 position of the
CC       reducing end forming a D-glucono-1,5-lactone residue (cf.
CC       EC 1.14.99.54).
//
ID   1.14.99.57
DE   Heme oxygenase (mycobilin-producing).
CA   (1) Protoheme + 3 reduced acceptor + 3 O(2) = mycobilin a + Fe(2+) +
CA   3 acceptor + 3 H(2)O.
CA   (2) Protoheme + 3 reduced acceptor + 3 O(2) = mycobilin b + Fe(2+) +
CA   3 acceptor + 3 H(2)O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Mycobacterium
CC       tuberculosis, is involved in heme degradation and iron utilization.
CC   -!- The enzyme binds two stacked protoheme molecules per monomer.
CC   -!- Unlike the canonical heme oxygenases, the enzyme does not release
CC       carbon monoxide or formaldehyde; instead, it forms unique products,
CC       named mycobilins, that retain the alpha-meso-carbon at the ring
CC       cleavage site as an aldehyde group.
CC   -!- EC 1.6.2.4.
//
ID   1.14.99.58
DE   Heme oxygenase (biliverdin-IX-beta and delta-forming).
CA   (1) Protoheme + 3 reduced acceptor + 3 O(2) = biliverdin-IX-delta + CO +
CA   Fe(2+) + 3 acceptor + 3 H(2)O.
CA   (2) Protoheme + 3 reduced acceptor + 3 O(2) = biliverdin-IX-beta + CO +
CA   Fe(2+) + 3 acceptor + 3 H(2)O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Pseudomonas aeruginosa,
CC       differs from EC 1.14.15.20 in that the heme substrate is rotated by
CC       approximately 110 degrees within the active site, resulting in
CC       cleavage at a different part of the ring.
CC   -!- It forms a mixture of about 70% biliverdin-IX-delta and 30%
CC       biliverdin-IX-beta.
//
ID   1.14.99.59
DE   Tryptamine 4-monooxygenase.
CA   Tryptamine + reduced acceptor + O(2) = 4-hydroxytryptamine + acceptor +
CA   H(2)O.
CF   Heme-thiolate.
CC   -!- Isolated from the fungus Psilocybe cubensis.
CC   -!- Involved in the biosynthesis of the psychoactive compound psilocybin.
