//
ID   1.16.3.2
DE   Bacterial non-heme ferritin.
CA   4 Fe(2+) + O(2) + 6 H(2)O = 4 (FeO(OH)) + 8 H(+).
CC   -!- Ferritins are intracellular iron-storage and detoxification proteins
CC       found in all kingdoms of life.
CC   -!- They are formed from two subunits that co-assemble in various ratios
CC       to form a spherical protein shell.
CC   -!- Thousands of mineralized iron atoms are stored within the core of the
CC       structure.
CC   -!- The product of dioxygen reduction by the bacterial non-heme ferritin
CC       is hydrogen peroxide, which is consumed in a subsequent reaction.
DR   P43707, FTN1_HAEIN ;  P0A9A0, FTNA_ECO57 ;  P0A999, FTNA_ECOL6 ;
DR   P0A998, FTNA_ECOLI ;  P0A9A1, FTNA_SHIFL ;  E1WS50, FTN_BACF6  ;
DR   P0CJ83, FTN_BACFR  ;  Q46106, FTN_CAMJE  ;  Q9ZLI1, FTN_HELPJ  ;
DR   P52093, FTN_HELPY  ;  Q2FFK2, FTN_STAA3  ;  Q2FWZ8, FTN_STAA8  ;
DR   Q2YU34, FTN_STAAB  ;  Q5HEN0, FTN_STAAC  ;  Q99SZ3, FTN_STAAM  ;
DR   Q7A4R2, FTN_STAAN  ;  Q6GFG4, FTN_STAAR  ;  Q6G840, FTN_STAAS  ;
DR   Q7A0H5, FTN_STAAW  ;  Q5HN41, FTN_STAEQ  ;  Q8CNP5, FTN_STAES  ;
DR   Q4L7K6, FTN_STAHJ  ;  Q49YT7, FTN_STAS1  ;
//
ID   1.16.3.3
DE   Manganese oxidase.
CA   4 Mn(2+) + 2 O(2) + 4 H(2)O = 4 MnO(2)+ + 8 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, which belongs to the multicopper oxidase family, is found
CC       in many bacterial strains.
CC   -!- It oxidizes soluble Mn(II) to insoluble manganese oxides.
CC   -!- Since the enzyme is localized to the outer surface of the cell,
CC       its activity usually results in encrustation of the cells by the
CC       oxides.
CC   -!- The physiological function of bacterial Mn(II) oxidation remains
CC       unclear.
//
ID   1.16.5.1
DE   Ascorbate ferrireductase (transmembrane).
AN   Cytochrome b(561).
CA   Ascorbate(Side 1) + Fe(III)(Side 2) = monodehydroascorbate(Side 1) +
CA   Fe(II)(Side 2).
CF   Heme.
CC   -!- A diheme cytochrome that transfers electrons across a single
CC       membrane, such as the outer membrane of the enterocyte, or the
CC       tonoplast membrane of the plant cell vacuole.
CC   -!- Acts on hexacyanoferrate(III) and other ferric chelates.
DR   Q8L856, ACFR1_ARATH;  Q9SWS1, ACFR2_ARATH;  Q67ZF6, ACFR3_ARATH;
DR   Q9C540, ACFR4_ARATH;
//
ID   1.16.8.1
DE   Cob(II)yrinic acid a,c-diamide reductase.
CA   2 cob(I)yrinic acid a,c-diamide + FMN + 2 H(+) = 2 cob(II)yrinic acid
CA   a,c-diamide + FMNH(2).
CC   -!- This enzyme also catalyzes the reduction of cob(II)yric acid,
CC       cob(II)inamide, cob(II)inamide phosphate, GDP-cob(II)inamide and
CC       cob(II)alamin although cob(II)yrinic acid a,c-diamide is thought to
CC       be the physiological substrate.
CC   -!- Also uses FAD and NADH but not NADPH.
//
ID   1.16.9.1
DE   Iron:rusticyanin reductase.
CA   Fe(II) + rusticyanin = Fe(III) + reduced rusticyanin.
CF   Heme c.
CC   -!- The enzyme in Acidithiobacillus ferrooxidans is a component of an
CC       electron transfer chain from Fe(II), comprising this enzyme,
CC       the copper protein rusticyanin, cytochrome c4, and cytochrome c
CC       oxidase (EC 1.9.3.1).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.16.98.1.
//
ID   1.16.98.1
DE   Transferred entry: 1.16.9.1.
//
ID   1.17.1.1
DE   CDP-4-dehydro-6-deoxyglucose reductase.
AN   CDP-4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose-3-dehydrogenase system.
AN   CDP-4-keto-6-deoxyglucose reductase.
AN   CDP-4-keto-deoxy-glucose reductase.
AN   Cytidine diphosphate 4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose-3-dehydrogenase.
AN   Cytidine diphospho-4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose reductase.
AN   NAD(P)H:CDP-4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose oxidoreductase.
CA   CDP-4-dehydro-3,6-dideoxy-D-glucose + NAD(P)(+) + H(2)O = CDP-4-dehydro-
CA   6-deoxy-D-glucose + NAD(P)H.
CC   -!- The enzyme consists of two proteins.
CC   -!- One forms an enzyme-bound adduct of the CDP-4-dehydro-6-deoxyglucose
CC       with pyridoxamine phosphate, in which the 3-hydroxy group has been
CC       removed.
CC   -!- The second catalyzes the reduction of this adduct by NAD(P)H and
CC       release of the CDP-4-dehydro-3,6-dideoxy-D-glucose and pyridoxamine
CC       phosphate.
//
ID   1.17.1.2
DE   Transferred entry: 1.17.7.4.
//
ID   1.17.1.3
DE   Leucoanthocyanidin reductase.
AN   Leucocyanidin reductase.
CA   (2R,3S)-catechin + NADP(+) + H(2)O = 2,3-trans-3,4-cis-leucocyanidin +
CA   NADPH.
CC   -!- Catalyzes the synthesis of catechin, catechin-4-beta-ol
CC       (leucocyanidin) and the related flavan-3-ols afzelechin and
CC       gallocatechin, which are initiating monomers in the synthesis of
CC       plant polymeric proanthocyanidins or condensed tannins.
CC   -!- While 2,3-trans-3,4-cis-leucocyanidin is the preferred flavan-3,4-
CC       diol substrate, 2,3-trans-3,4-cis-leucodelphinidin and 2,3-trans-3,
CC       4-cis-leucopelargonidin can also act as substrates, but more slowly.
CC   -!- NADH can replace NADPH but is oxidized more slowly.
DR   Q84V83, LAR_DESUN  ;
//
ID   1.17.1.4
DE   Xanthine dehydrogenase.
AN   NAD-xanthine dehydrogenase.
AN   Xanthine oxidoreductase.
AN   Xanthine-NAD oxidoreductase.
AN   Xanthine/NAD(+) oxidoreductase.
CA   Xanthine + NAD(+) + H(2)O = urate + NADH.
CF   FAD; Iron-sulfur; Mo cation.
CC   -!- Acts on a variety of purines and aldehydes, including hypoxanthine.
CC   -!- The mammalian enzyme can also convert all-trans retinol to all-trans-
CC       retinoate, while the substrate is bound to a retinoid-binding
CC       protein.
CC   -!- The enzyme from eukaryotes contains [2Fe-2S], FAD and a molybdenum
CC       center.
CC   -!- The mammallian enzyme predominantly exists as the NAD-dependent
CC       dehydrogenase (EC 1.17.1.4).
CC   -!- During purification the enzyme is largely converted to an O(2)-
CC       dependent form, EC 1.17.3.2.
CC   -!- The conversion can be triggered by several mechanisms, including the
CC       oxidation of cysteine thiols to form disulfide bonds (which can be
CC       catalyzed by EC 1.8.4.7 in the presence of glutathione disulfide) or
CC       limited proteolysis, which results in irreversible conversion.
CC   -!- The conversion can also occur in vivo.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.2.1.37 and EC 1.1.1.204.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00484;
