ID   1.17.99.2
DE   Ethylbenzene hydroxylase.
AN   Ethylbenzene dehydrogenase.
CA   Ethylbenzene + H(2)O + acceptor = (S)-1-phenylethanol + reduced acceptor.
CF   Heme; Iron-sulfur; Mo-molybdopterin.
CC   -!- Involved in the anaerobic catabolism of ethylbenzene by denitrifying
CC       bacteria.
CC   -!- Ethylbenzene is the preferred substrate; the enzyme from some strains
CC       oxidizes propylbenzene, 1-ethyl-4-fluorobenzene, 3-methylpent-2-ene
CC       and ethylidenecyclohexane.
CC   -!- Toluene is not oxidized.
CC   -!- p-benzoquinone or ferrocenium can act as electron acceptor.
//
ID   1.17.99.3
DE   3-alpha,7-alpha,12-alpha-trihydroxy-5-beta-cholestanoyl-CoA
DE   24-hydroxylase.
AN   3-alpha,7-alpha,12-alpha-trihydroxy-5-beta-cholestan-26-oate
AN   24-hydroxylase.
AN   3-alpha,7-alpha,12-alpha-trihydroxy-5-beta-cholestanoyl-CoA oxidase.
AN   THC-CoA oxidase.
AN   THCA-CoA oxidase.
AN   Trihydroxycoprostanoyl-CoA oxidase.
CA   (25R)-3-alpha,7-alpha,12-alpha-trihydroxy-5-beta-cholestan-26-oyl-CoA +
CA   H(2)O + acceptor = (24R,25R)-3-alpha,7-alpha,12-alpha,24-tetrahydroxy-5-
CA   beta-cholestan-26-oyl-CoA + reduced acceptor.
CC   -!- Requires ATP.
CC   -!- The reaction in mammals possibly involves dehydrogenation to give a
CC       24(25)-double bond followed by hydration.
CC   -!- However, in amphibians such as the Oriental fire-bellied toad
CC       (Bombina orientalis), it is probable that the product is formed via
CC       direct hydroxylation of the saturated side chain of (25R)-3-alpha,7-
CC       alpha,12-alpha-trihydroxy-5-beta-cholestan-26-oate and not via
CC       hydration of a 24(25) double bond.
CC   -!- In microsomes, the free acid is preferred to the coenzyme A ester,
CC       whereas in mitochondria, the coenzyme A ester is preferred to the
CC       free-acid form of the substrate.
DR   Q99424, ACOX2_HUMAN;  Q9QXD1, ACOX2_MOUSE;  O02767, ACOX2_RABIT;
DR   P97562, ACOX2_RAT  ;
//
ID   1.17.99.4
DE   Uracil/thymine dehydrogenase.
AN   Uracil dehydrogenase.
AN   Uracil oxidase.
AN   Uracil-thymine oxidase.
CA   (1) Uracil + H(2)O + acceptor = barbiturate + reduced acceptor.
CA   (2) Thymine + H(2)O + acceptor = 5-methylbarbiturate + reduced acceptor.
CC   -!- Forms part of the oxidative pyrimidine-degrading pathway in some
CC       microorganisms, along with EC 3.5.2.1 and EC 3.5.1.95.
CC   -!- Mammals, plants and other microorganisms utilize the reductive
CC       pathway, comprising EC 1.3.1.1 or EC 1.3.1.2, EC 3.5.2.2 and
CC       EC 3.5.1.6, with the ultimate degradation products being an L-amino
CC       acid, NH(3) and CO(2).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.99.19 and EC 1.2.99.1.
//
ID   1.17.99.5
DE   Transferred entry: 1.1.1.395.
