ID   2.1.1.150
DE   Isoflavone 7-O-methyltransferase.
CA   S-adenosyl-L-methionine + a 7-hydroxyisoflavone = S-adenosyl-L-
CA   homocysteine + a 7-methoxyisoflavone.
CC   -!- The enzyme from alfalfa can methylate daidzein, genistein and
CC       6,7,4'-trihydroxyisoflavone but not flavones or flavanones.
DR   O22308, 7OMT6_MEDSA;  O24529, 7OMT8_MEDSA;  O22309, 7OMT9_MEDSA;
DR   Q84KK5, D7OMT_GLYEC;
//
ID   2.1.1.151
DE   Cobalt-factor II C(20)-methyltransferase.
AN   Cobalt-precorrin-2 C(20)-methyltransferase.
CA   S-adenosyl-L-methionine + cobalt-factor II = S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine +
CA   cobalt-factor III.
CC   -!- Involved in the anaerobic biosynthesis of vitamin B12.
DR   Q58181, CBIL_METJA ;  Q05593, CBIL_SALTY ;
//
ID   2.1.1.152
DE   Precorrin-6A synthase (deacetylating).
AN   Precorrin-6X synthase (deacetylating).
CA   S-adenosyl-L-methionine + precorrin-5 + H(2)O = S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine
CA   + precorrin-6A + acetate.
CC   -!- In the aerobic cobalamin biosythesis pathway, four enzymes are
CC       involved in the conversion of precorrin-3A to precorrin-6A.
CC   -!- The first of the four steps is carried out by EC 1.14.13.83 (CobG),
CC       yielding precorrin-3B as the product.
CC   -!- This is followed by three methylation reactions, which introduce a
CC       methyl group at C-17 (CobJ; EC 2.1.1.131), C-11 (CobM; 2.1.1.133) and
CC       C-1 (CobF; EC 2.1.1.152) of the macrocycle, giving rise to precorrin-
CC       4, precorrin-5 and precorrin-6A, respectively.
DR   P21636, COBF_PSEDE ;
//
ID   2.1.1.153
DE   Vitexin 2''-O-rhamnoside 7-O-methyltransferase.
CA   S-adenosyl-L-methionine + vitexin 2''-O-beta-L-rhamnoside = S-adenosyl-L-
CA   homocysteine + 7-O-methylvitexin 2''-O-beta-L-rhamnoside.
CC   -!- The flavonoids vitexin and isovitexin 2''-O-arabinoside do not act as
CC       substrates for the enzyme from oats (Avena sativa).
//
ID   2.1.1.154
DE   Isoliquiritigenin 2'-O-methyltransferase.
AN   Chalcone OMT.
AN   CHMT.
CA   S-adenosyl-L-methionine + isoliquiritigenin = S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine +
CA   2'-O-methylisoliquiritigenin.
CC   -!- Not identical to EC 2.1.1.65.
CC   -!- While EC 2.1.1.154 can use licodione as a substrate, EC 2.1.1.65
CC       cannot use isoliquiritigenin as a substrate.
DR   P93324, CHOMT_MEDSA;
//
ID   2.1.1.155
DE   Kaempferol 4'-O-methyltransferase.
AN   F 4'-OMT.
AN   S-adenosyl-L-methionine:flavonoid 4'-O-methyltransferase.
CA   S-adenosyl-L-methionine + kaempferol = S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine +
CA   kaempferide.
CC   -!- The enzyme acts on the hydroxy group in the 4'-position of some
CC       flavones, flavanones and isoflavones.
CC   -!- Kaempferol, apigenin and kaempferol triglucoside are substrates,
CC       as is genistein, which reacts more slowly.
CC   -!- Compounds with an hydroxy group in the 3' and 4' positions, such as
CC       quercetin and eriodictyol, do not act as substrates.
CC   -!- Similar to EC 2.1.1.75 and EC 2.1.1.83.
//
ID   2.1.1.156
DE   Glycine/sarcosine N-methyltransferase.
AN   Glycine sarcosine N-methyltransferase.
AN   Glycine-sarcosine methyltransferase.
AN   GMT.
AN   GSMT.
CA   2 S-adenosyl-L-methionine + glycine = 2 S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine +
CA   N,N-dimethylglycine.
CC   -!- Cells of the oxygen-evolving halotolerant cyanobacterium Aphanocthece
CC       halophytica synthesize betaine from glycine by a three-step
CC       methylation process.
CC   -!- This is the first enzyme and it leads to the formation of either
CC       sarcosine or N,N-dimethylglycine, which is further methylated to
CC       yield betaine (N,N,N-trimethylglycine) by the action of EC 2.1.1.157.
CC   -!- Differs from EC 2.1.1.20, as it can further methylate the product of
CC       the first reaction.
CC   -!- Acetate, dimethylglycine and S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine can inhibit
CC       the reaction.
DR   Q9KJ20, GSDMT_ACTHA;  Q83WC4, GSMT_APHHA ;  Q9KJ22, GSMT_HALHR ;
DR   Q7U4Z8, GSMT_SYNPX ;
//
ID   2.1.1.157
DE   Sarcosine/dimethylglycine N-methyltransferase.
AN   ApDMT.
AN   Sarcosine dimethylglycine N-methyltransferase.
AN   Sarcosine-dimethylglycine methyltransferase.
AN   SDMT.
CA   2 S-adenosyl-L-methionine + sarcosine = 2 S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine +
CA   betaine.
CC   -!- Cells of the oxygen-evolving halotolerant cyanobacterium Aphanocthece
CC       halophytica synthesize betaine from glycine by a three-step
CC       methylation process.
CC   -!- The first enzyme, EC 2.1.1.156, leads to the formation of either
CC       sarcosine or N,N-dimethylglycine, which is further methylated to
CC       yield betaine (N,N,N-trimethylglycine) by the action of this enzyme.
CC   -!- Both of these enzymes can catalyze the formation of
CC       N,N-dimethylglycine from sarcosine.
CC   -!- The reactions are strongly inhibited by S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine.
DR   Q9KJ20, GSDMT_ACTHA;  Q83WC3, SDMT_APHHA ;  Q9KJ21, SDMT_HALHR ;
//
ID   2.1.1.158
DE   7-methylxanthosine synthase.
AN   Xanthosine methyltransferase.
AN   XMT.
AN   XRS1.
CA   S-adenosyl-L-methionine + xanthosine = S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine +
CA   7-methylxanthosine.
CC   -!- The enzyme is specific for xanthosine, as XMP and xanthine cannot act
CC       as substrates.
CC   -!- The enzyme does not have N(1)- or N(3)- methylation activity.
CC   -!- This is the first methylation step in the production of caffeine.
DR   Q9AVK0, XMT1_COFAR ;  A4GE69, XMT1_COFCA ;
//
ID   2.1.1.159
DE   Theobromine synthase.
AN   Monomethylxanthine methyltransferase.
AN   MXMT.
CA   S-adenosyl-L-methionine + 7-methylxanthine = S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine +
CA   3,7-dimethylxanthine.
CC   -!- Can also catalyze the conversion of paraxanthine into caffeine,
CC       although the paraxanthine pathway is considered to be a minor pathway
CC       for caffeine biosynthesis.
CC   -!- This is the third enzyme in the caffeine biosynthesis pathway.
DR   Q8H0G0, CTS2_COFAR ;  Q9AVJ9, MXMT1_COFAR;  Q84PP7, MXMT2_COFAR;
//
ID   2.1.1.160
DE   Caffeine synthase.
AN   3N-methyltransferase.
AN   Dimethylxanthine methyltransferase.
CA   (1) S-adenosyl-L-methionine + 3,7-dimethylxanthine = S-adenosyl-L-
CA   homocysteine + 1,3,7-trimethylxanthine.
CA   (2) S-adenosyl-L-methionine + 1,7-dimethylxanthine = S-adenosyl-L-
CA   homocysteine + 1,3,7-trimethylxanthine.
CA   (3) S-adenosyl-L-methionine + 7-methylxanthine = S-adenosyl-L-
CA   homocysteine + 3,7-dimethylxanthine.
CC   -!- Paraxanthine is the best substrate for this enzyme but the
CC       paraxanthine pathway is considered to be a minor pathway for caffeine
CC       biosynthesis.
DR   Q8H0D3, CCS1_COFAR ;  Q8H0D2, DXMT1_COFAR;  A4GE70, DXMT1_COFCA;
DR   Q9FZN8, TCS1_CAMSI ;  Q68CM3, TCS2_CAMSI ;
//
ID   2.1.1.161
DE   Dimethylglycine N-methyltransferase.
AN   DMT.
CA   S-adenosyl-L-methionine + N,N-dimethylglycine = S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine
CA   + betaine.
CC   -!- This enzyme, from the marine cyanobacterium Synechococcus sp. strain
CC       WH8102, differs from EC 2.1.1.157 in that it cannot use sarcosine as
CC       an alternative substrate.
CC   -!- Betaine is a 'compatible solute' that enables cyanobacteria to cope
CC       with osmotic stress by maintaining a positive cellular turgor.
DR   Q7U4Z9, SDMT_SYNPX ;
//
ID   2.1.1.162
DE   Glycine/sarcosine/dimethylglycine N-methyltransferase.
AN   Glycine sarcosine dimethylglycine N-methyltransferase.
AN   GSDMT.
CA   3 S-adenosyl-L-methionine + glycine = 3 S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine +
CA   betaine.
CC   -!- Unlike EC 2.1.1.156, EC 2.1.1.157 and EC 2.1.1.161, this enzyme, from
CC       the halophilic methanoarchaeon Methanohalophilus portucalensis,
CC       can methylate glycine and all of its intermediates to form the
CC       compatible solute betaine.
//
ID   2.1.1.163
DE   Demethylmenaquinone methyltransferase.
AN   2-demethylmenaquinone methyltransferase.
AN   2-heptaprenyl-1,4-naphthoquinone methyltransferase.
AN   Demethylmenaquinone C-methylase.
CA   A demethylmenaquinol + S-adenosyl-L-methionine = a menaquinol +
CA   S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the last step in menaquinone biosynthesis.
CC   -!- It is able to accept substrates with varying polyprenyl side chain
CC       length (the chain length is determined by polyprenyl diphosphate
CC       synthase).
CC   -!- The enzyme from Escherichia coli also catalyzes the conversion of
CC       2-methoxy-6-octaprenyl-1,4-benzoquinone to 5-methoxy-2-methyl-3-
CC       octaprenyl-1,4-benzoquinone during the biosynthesis of ubiquinone.
CC   -!- The enzyme probably acts on menaquinol rather than menaquinone.
