ID   2.3.1.232
DE   Methanol O-anthraniloyltransferase.
CA   Anthraniloyl-CoA + methanol = CoA + O-methyl anthranilate.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Concord grape (Vitis labrusca) is solely responsible
CC       for the production of O-methyl anthranilate, an important aroma and
CC       flavor compound in the grape.
CC   -!- The enzyme has a broad substrate specificity, and can use a range of
CC       alcohols with substantial activity, the best being butanol, benzyl
CC       alcohol, iso-pentanol, octanol and 2-propanol.
CC   -!- It can use benzoyl-CoA and acetyl-CoA as acyl donors with lower
CC       efficiency.
CC   -!- In addition to O-methyl anthranilate, the enzyme might be responsible
CC       for the production of ethyl butanoate, methyl-3-hydroxy butanoate and
CC       ethyl-3-hydroxy butanoate, which are present in large quantities in
CC       the grapes.
CC   -!- Also catalyzes EC 2.3.1.196.
DR   Q3ZPN4, ACMAT_VITLA;
//
ID   2.3.1.233
DE   1,3,6,8-tetrahydroxynaphthalene synthase.
AN   THNS.
CA   5 malonyl-CoA = 1,3,6,8-tetrahydroxynaphthalene + 5 CoA + 5 CO(2) +
CA   H(2)O.
CC   -!- Isolated from the fungus Colletotrichum lagenarium, and the bacteria
CC       Streptomyces coelicolor and Streptomyces peucetius.
CC   -!- It only uses malonyl-CoA, without invovement of acetyl-CoA.
DR   B0FYK7, RPPA_STRC0 ;  Q9FCA7, RPPA_STRCO ;  Q54240, RPPA_STRGR ;
