ID   2.3.1.252
DE   Mycolipanoate synthase.
AN   Mycolipanoic acid synthase.
CA   A long-chain acyl-CoA + 3 (S)-methylmalonyl-CoA + 6 NADPH + holo-
CA   [mycolipanoate synthase] = mycolipanoyl-[mycolipanoate synthase] + 4 CoA
CA   + 3 CO(2) + 6 NADP(+) + 3 H(2)O.
CC   -!- This mycobacterial enzyme accepts long-chain fatty acyl groups from
CC       their CoA esters and extends them by incorporation of three
CC       methylmalonyl (but not malonyl) residues, forming trimethyl-branched
CC       fatty-acids such as (2S,4S,6S)-2,4,6-trimethyl-tetracosanoate (C(27)-
CC       mycolipanoate).
CC   -!- Since the enzyme lacks a thioesterase domain, the product remains
CC       bound to the enzyme and requires additional enzyme(s) for removal.
DR   A0A089QRB9, MSL3_MYCTU ;
//
ID   2.3.1.253
DE   Phloroglucinol synthase.
CA   3 malonyl-CoA = phloroglucinol + 3 CO(2) + 3 CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Pseudomonas protegens
CC       Pf-5, is a type III polyketide synthase.
CC   -!- The mechanism involves the cyclization of an activated 3,5-
CC       dioxoheptanedioate intermediate.
CC   -!- The enzyme exhibits broad substrate specificity, and can accept C(4)-
CC       C(12) aliphatic acyl-CoAs and phenylacetyl-CoA as the starter
CC       molecules, forming C(6)-polyoxoalkylated alpha-pyrones by sequential
CC       condensation with malonyl-CoA.
//
ID   2.3.1.254
DE   N-terminal methionine N(alpha)-acetyltransferase NatB.
CA   (1) Acetyl-CoA + an N-terminal L-methionyl-L-asparaginyl-[protein] =
CA   an N-terminal N(alpha)-acetyl-L-methionyl-L-asparginyl-[protein] + CoA.
CA   (2) Acetyl-CoA + an N-terminal L-methionyl-L-glutaminyl-[protein] =
CA   an N-terminal N(alpha)-acetyl-L-methionyl-L-glutaminyl-[protein] + CoA.
CA   (3) Acetyl-CoA + an N-terminal L-methionyl-L-aspartyl-[protein] =
CA   an N-terminal N(alpha)-acetyl-L-methionyl-L-aspartyl-[protein] + CoA.
CA   (4) Acetyl-CoA + an N-terminal L-methionyl-L-glutamyl-[protein] =
CA   an N-terminal N(alpha)-acetyl-L-methionyl-L-glutamyl-[protein] + CoA.
CC   -!- N-terminal acetylases (NATs) catalyze the covalent attachment of an
CC       acetyl moiety from acetyl-CoA to the free alpha-amino group at the
CC       N-terminus of a protein.
CC   -!- This irreversible modification neutralizes the positive charge at the
CC       N-terminus and makes the N-terminal residue larger and more
CC       hydrophobic, and may also play a role in membrane targeting and gene
CC       silencing.
CC   -!- The NatB complex is found in all eukaryotic organisms,
CC       and specifically targets N-terminal L-methionine residues attached to
CC       Asn, Asp, Gln, or Glu residues at the second position.
