CC   -!- N-terminal-acetylases (NATs) catalyze the covalent attachment of an
CC       acetyl moiety from acetyl-CoA to the free alpha-amino group at the
CC       N-terminus of a protein.
CC   -!- This irreversible modification neutralizes the positive charge at the
CC       N-terminus, makes the N-terminal residue larger and more hydrophobic,
CC       and prevents its removal by hydrolysis.
CC   -!- NatF is found only in higher eukaryotes, and is absent from yeast.
CC   -!- Unlike other Nat systems the enzyme is located in the Golgi
CC       apparatus.
CC   -!- It faces the cytosolic side of intracellular membranes,
CC       and specifically acetylates transmembrane proteins whose N termini
CC       face the cytosol.
CC   -!- NatF targets N-terminal L-methionine residues attached to Lys, Ser,
CC       Val, Leu, Gln, Ile, Tyr and Thr residues.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 2.3.1.88.
