ID   2.3.1.260
DE   Tetracycline polyketide synthase.
CA   Malonamoyl-[OxyC acyl-carrier protein] + 8 malonyl-CoA = 18-carbamoyl-
CA   3,5,7,9,11,13,15,17-octaoxooctadecanoyl-[OxyC acyl-carrier protein] +
CA   8 CO(2) + 8 CoA.
CC   -!- The synthesis, in the bacterium Streptomyces rimosus of the
CC       tetracycline antibiotics core skeleton requires a minimal polyketide
CC       synthase (PKS) consisting of a ketosynthase (KS), a chain length
CC       factor (CLF), and an acyl-carrier protein (ACP).
CC   -!- Initiation involves an amide-containing starter unit that becomes the
CC       C-2 amide that is present in the tetracycline compounds.
CC   -!- Following the initiation, the PKS catalyzes the iterative
CC       condensation of 8 malonyl-CoA molecules to yield the polyketide
CC       backbone of tetracycline.
CC   -!- Throughout the proccess, the nascent chain is attached to the
CC       OxyC acyl-carrier protein.
//
ID   2.3.1.261
DE   (4-hydroxyphenyl)alkanoate synthase.
CA   (1) 4-hydroxybenzoyl-[(4-hydroxyphenyl)alkanoate synthase] + 8 malonyl-
CA   CoA + 16 NADPH = 17-(4-hydroxyphenyl)heptadecanoyl-
CA   [(4-hydroxyphenyl)alkanoate synthase] + 8 CO(2) + 8 CoA + 16 NADP(+) +
CA   8 H(2)O.
CA   (2) 4-hydroxybenzoyl-[(4-hydroxyphenyl)alkanoate synthase] + 9 malonyl-
CA   CoA + 18 NADPH = 19-(4-hydroxyphenyl)nonadecanoyl-
CA   [(4-hydroxyphenyl)alkanoate synthase] + 9 CO(2) + 9 CoA + 18 NADP(+) +
CA   9 H(2)O.
CC   -!- The enzyme is part of the biosynthetic pathway of phenolphthiocerol,
CC       a lipid that serves as a virulence factor of pathogenic mycobacteria.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the elongation of 4-hydroxybenzoate that is loaded on
CC       its acyl-carrier domain to form (4-hydroxyphenyl)alkanoate
CC       intermediates.
CC   -!- The enzyme adds either 8 or 9 malonyl-CoA units, resulting in
CC       formation of 17-(4-hydroxyphenyl)heptadecanoate or
CC       19-(4-hydroxyphenyl)nonadecanoate, respectively.
CC   -!- As the enzyme lacks a thioesterase domain, the product remains loaded
CC       on the acyl-carrier domain at the end of catalysis, and has to be
CC       hydrolyzed by an as-yet unknown mechanism.
//
ID   2.3.1.262
