ID   2.3.1.n12
DE   Sinapoyl-beta-D-glucose:anthocyanin sinapoyltransferase.
CA   1-O-sinapoyl-beta-D-glucose + cyanidin 3-O-(6-O-(4-O-beta-D-glucosyl-p-
CA   coumaroyl)-2-O-(beta-D-xylosyl)-beta-D-glucosyl)-5-O-(6-O-malonyl-beta-D-
CA   glucoside) = D-glucose + cyanidin 3-O-(6-O-(4-O-beta-D-glucosyl-p-
CA   coumaroyl)-2-O-(2-O-sinapoyl-beta-D-xylosyl)-beta-D-glucosyl)-5-O-(6-O-
CA   malonyl-beta-D-glucoside).
CC   -!- The product is the most abundant anthocyanin in the plant Arabidopsis
CC       thaliana.
CC   -!- The enzyme likely sinapoylates other anthocyanins in the plant.
//
ID   2.3.2.1
DE   D-glutamyltransferase.
AN   D-glutamyl transpeptidase.
CA   (1) D-glutamine + D-glutamate = NH(3) + gamma-D-glutamyl-D-glutamate.
CA   (2) L(or D)-glutamine + (gamma-D-glutamyl)(n)-[peptide] = NH(3) + (gamma-
CA   D-glutamyl)(n+1)-[peptide].
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes two reactions; the first is the transfer of a
CC       glutamyl residue from L- or D-glutamine to D-glutamate via a gamma
CC       linkage, forming gamma-glutamyl-D-glutamate, and the second is the
CC       transfer of additional glutamyl residues to the peptide, extending
CC       the polypeptide chain.
//
ID   2.3.2.2
DE   Gamma-glutamyltransferase.
AN   Gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase.
AN   Gamma-glutamyltranspeptidase.
AN   Glutamyl transpeptidase.
CA   A (5-L-glutamyl)-peptide + an amino acid = a peptide + a 5-L-glutamyl
CA   amino acid.
CC   -!- The mammlian enzyme is part of the cell antioxidant defense
CC       mechanism.
CC   -!- It initiates extracellular glutathione (GSH) breakdown, provides
CC       cells with a local cysteine supply and contributes to maintain
CC       intracelular GSH levels.
CC   -!- The protein also has EC 3.4.19.13 activity.
CC   -!- The enzyme consists of two chains that are created by the proteolytic
CC       cleavage of a single precursor polypeptide.
CC   -!- The N-terminal L-threonine of the C-terminal subunit functions as the
CC       active site for both the cleavage and the hydrolysis reactions.
