ID   2.3.3.17
DE   Methylthioalkylmalate synthase.
CA   An omega-(methylthio)-2-oxoalkanoate + acetyl-CoA + H(2)O = a 2-(omega-
CA   methylthio)alkylmalate + CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the plant Arabidopsis thaliana,
CC       is involved in the L-methionine side-chain elongation pathway,
CC       forming substrates for the biosynthesis of aliphatic glucosinolates.
CC   -!- Two forms are known - MAM1 catalyzes only only the first two rounds
CC       of methionine chain elongation, while MAM3 catalyzes all six cycles,
CC       up to formation of L-hexahomomethionine.
DR   Q9FG67, MAM1_ARATH ;  Q8VX04, MAM2_ARATH ;  Q9FN52, MAM3_ARATH ;
//
ID   2.3.3.18
DE   2-phosphinomethylmalate synthase.
CA   Acetyl-CoA + H(2)O + 3-(hydroxyphosphinoyl)pyruvate =
CA   phosphinomethylmalate + CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Streptomyces
CC       hygroscopicus, participates in the pathway for bialaphos
CC       biosynthesis.
CC   -!- It requires a divalent metal ion and can also act on oxaloacetate.
DR   Q9LCB4, PMMS_STRHY ;
//
ID   2.3.3.19
DE   2-phosphonomethylmalate synthase.
AN   2-phosphinomethylmalic acid synthase.
AN   PMM synthase.
CA   3-phosphonopyruvate + acetyl-CoA + H(2)O = (R)-2-(phosphonomethyl)malate
CA   + CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from several Streptomyces species, participates
CC       in the biosynthesis of certain phosphonate antibiotics.
CC   -!- The enzyme is analogous to EC 2.3.3.1.
DR   Q0ZQ46, FRBC_STRR3 ;
//
ID   2.3.3.20
DE   Acyl-CoA:acyl-CoA alkyltransferase.
CA   2 an acyl-CoA + H(2)O = a (2R)-2-alkyl-3-oxoalkanoate + 2 CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in certain bacterial species, catalyzes a head-
CC       to-head non-decarboxylative Claisen condensation of two acyl-CoA
CC       molecules, resulting in formation of a 2-alkyl-3-oxoalkanoic acid.
CC   -!- It is part of a pathway for the production of olefins.
//
ID   2.4.1.1
DE   Glycogen phosphorylase.
AN   Amylophosphorylase.
AN   Muscle phosphorylase a and b.
AN   Polyphosphorylase.
CA   ((1->4)-alpha-D-glucosyl)(n) + phosphate = ((1->4)-alpha-D-glucosyl)(n-1)
CA   + alpha-D-glucose 1-phosphate.
CC   -!- This entry covers several enzymes from different sources that act in
CC       vivo on different forms of (1->4)-alpha-D-glucans.
CC   -!- Some of these enzymes catalyze the first step in the degradation of
CC       large branched glycan polymers - the phosphorolytic cleavage of
CC       alpha-1,4-glucosidic bonds from the non-reducing ends of linear
CC       poly(1->4)-alpha-D-glucosyl chains within the polymers.
CC   -!- The enzyme stops when it reaches the fourth residue away from an
CC       alpha-1,6 branching point, leaving a highly branched core known as a
CC       limit dextrin.
CC   -!- The description (accepted name) of the enzyme should be modified for
CC       each specific instance by substituting 'glycogen' with the name of
CC       the natural substrate, e.g. maltodextrin phosphorylase, starch
CC       phosphorylase, etc.
