ID   2.4.1.337
DE   1,2-diacylglycerol 3-alpha-glucosyltransferase.
AN   1,2-diacylglycerol 3-glucosyltransferase.
AN   UDP-glucose-diacylglycerol glucosyltransferase.
AN   Uridine diphosphoglucose-diacylglycerol glucosyltransferase.
CA   UDP-alpha-D-glucose + a 1,2-diacyl-sn-glycerol = UDP + a 1,2-diacyl-3-
CA   O-(alpha-D-glucopyranosyl)-sn-glycerol.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Acholeplasma laidlawii, which lacks a
CC       celll wall, produces the major non-bilayer lipid in the organism.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Agrobacterium tumefaciens acts under
CC       phosphate deprivation, generating glycolipids as surrogates for
CC       phospholipids.
CC   -!- The enzyme belongs to the GT4 family of configuration-retaining
CC       glycosyltransferases.
CC   -!- Many diacylglycerols with long-chain acyl groups can act as
CC       acceptors.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 2.4.1.336.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 2.4.1.157.
DR   Q93P60, AMGDS_ACHLA;  Q8CWR6, AMGDS_STRR6;  Q8KQL6, PADGT_ACHLA;
//
ID   2.4.1.338
DE   Validoxylamine A glucosyltransferase.
CA   UDP-alpha-D-glucose + validoxylamine A = UDP + validamycin A.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Streptomyces
CC       hygroscopicus subsp. limoneus, catalyzes the ultimate step in the
CC       biosynthesis of the antifungal agent validamycin A.
DR   H2K893, VALG_STRHJ ;  Q15JF5, VLDK_STRHL ;
//
ID   2.4.1.339
DE   Beta-1,2-mannobiose phosphorylase.
CA   Beta-D-mannopyranosyl-(1->2)-D-mannopyranose + phosphate =
CA   D-mannopyranose + alpha-D-mannose 1-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, originally characterized from the thermophilic anaerobic
CC       bacterium Thermoanaerobacter sp. X514, catalyzes a reversible
CC       reaction.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 2.4.1.340.
//
ID   2.4.1.340
DE   1,2-beta-oligomannan phosphorylase.
CA   ((1->2)-beta-D-mannosyl)(n) + phosphate = ((1->2)-beta-D-mannosyl)(n-1) +
CA   alpha-D-mannose 1-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, originally characterized from the thermophilic anaerobic
CC       bacterium Thermoanaerobacter sp. X514, catalyzes a reversible
CC       reaction.
CC   -!- In the synthetic direction it produces oligosaccharides with a degree
CC       of polymerization (DP) of 3, 4 and 5.
CC   -!- The phosphorolysis reaction proceeds to completion, although activity
CC       is highest when the substrate has at least three residues.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 2.4.1.339.
//
ID   2.4.1.341
DE   Alpha-1,2-colitosyltransferase.
CA   GDP-beta-L-colitose + beta-D-galactopyranosyl-(1->3)-N-acetyl-D-
CA   glucosamine = GDP + alpha-L-colitosyl-(1->2)-beta-D-galactosyl-(1->3)-N-
CA   acetyl-D-glucosamine.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Escherichia coli O55:H7,
CC       participates in the biosynthesis of an O-antigen.
CC   -!- The reaction involves anomeric inversion, and does not require any
CC       metal ions.
CC   -!- The enzyme is highly specific toward the acceptor, exclusively
CC       recognizing lacto-N-biose, but can accept GDP-L-fucose as the donor
CC       with almost the same activity as with GDP-beta-L-colitose.
//
ID   2.4.1.342
DE   Alpha-maltose-1-phosphate synthase.
CA   ADP-alpha-D-glucose + alpha-D-glucose-1-phosphate = ADP + alpha-maltose-
