ID   2.7.7.62
DE   Adenosylcobinamide-phosphate guanylyltransferase.
AN   Adenosylcobinamide kinase/adenosylcobinamide-phosphate
AN   guanylyltransferase.
AN   AdoCbi kinase/AdoCbi-phosphate guanylyltransferase.
AN   GTP:adenosylcobinamide-phosphate guanylyltransferase.
CA   GTP + adenosylcobinamide phosphate = diphosphate + adenosylcobinamide-
CA   GDP.
CC   -!- In Salmonella typhimurium LT2, under anaerobic conditions, CobU
CC       (EC 2.7.7.62 and EC 2.7.1.156), CobT (EC 2.4.2.21),
CC       CobC (EC 3.1.3.73) and CobS (EC 2.7.8.26) catalyze reactions in the
CC       nucleotide loop assembly pathway, which convert adenosylcobinamide
CC       (AdoCbi) into adenosylcobalamin (AdoCbl).
CC   -!- CobT and CobC are involved in 5,6-dimethylbenzimidazole activation
CC       whereby 5,6-dimethylbenzimidazole is converted to its riboside,
CC       alpha-ribazole.
CC   -!- The second branch of the nuclotide loop assembly pathway is the
CC       cobinamide (Cbi) activation branch where AdoCbi or
CC       adenosylcobinamide-phosphate is converted to the activated
CC       intermediate AdoCbi-GDP by the bifunctional enzyme CobU.
CC   -!- The final step in adenosylcobalamin biosynthesis is the condensation
CC       of AdoCbi-GDP with alpha-ribazole, which is catalyzed by CobS
CC       (EC 2.7.8.26), to yield adenosylcobalamin.
CC   -!- CobU is a bifunctional enzyme that has both kinase (EC 2.7.1.156) and
CC       guanylyltransferase (EC 2.7.7.62) activities.
CC   -!- However, both activities are not required at all times.
CC   -!- The kinase activity has been proposed to function only when
CC       S.typhimurium is assimilating cobinamide whereas the
CC       guanylyltransferase activity is required for both assimilation of
CC       exogenous cobinamide and for de novo synthesis of adenosylcobalamin.
CC   -!- The guanylyltransferase reaction is a two-stage reaction with
CC       formation of a CobU-GMP intermediate.
DR   Q9I466, COBP_PSEAE ;  P29931, COBP_PSEDE ;  P0AE77, COBU_ECO57 ;
DR   P0AE76, COBU_ECOLI ;  Q05599, COBU_SALTY ;  Q58517, COBY_METJA ;
//
ID   2.7.7.63
DE   Transferred entry: 6.3.1.20.
//
ID   2.7.7.64
DE   UTP-monosaccharide-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase.
AN   UDP-sugar pyrophosphorylase.
AN   USP.
CA   UTP + a monosaccharide 1-phosphate = diphosphate + UDP-monosaccharide.
CF   Mn(2+) or Mg(2+).
CC   -!- The reaction can occur in either direction and it has been postulated
CC       that MgUTP and Mg-diphosphate are the actual substrates.
CC   -!- Catalyzes the formation of UDP-Glc, UDP-Gal, UDP-GlcA, UDP-L-Ara and
CC       UDP-Xyl, showing broad substrate specificity toward monosaccharide
CC       1-phosphates.
CC   -!- Mannose 1-phosphate, L-fucose 1-phosphate and glucose 6-phosphate are
CC       not substrates and UTP cannot be replaced by other nucleotide
CC       triphosphates.
DR   Q09WE7, USP1_SOYBN ;  Q9C5I1, USP_ARATH  ;  Q0GZS3, USP_CUCME  ;
DR   A2YGP6, USP_ORYSI  ;  Q5Z8Y4, USP_ORYSJ  ;  Q5W915, USP_PEA    ;
//
ID   2.7.7.65
DE   Diguanylate cyclase.
AN   DGC.
CA   2 GTP = 2 diphosphate + cyclic di-3',5'-guanylate.
CF   Mg(2+); Mn(2+).
CC   -!- Can be activated by BeF3, a phosphoryl mimic, which results in
CC       dimerization.
CC   -!- Dimerization is required but is not sufficient for diguanylate-
CC       cyclase activity.
CC   -!- Cyclic di-3',5'-guanylate is an intracellular signaling molecule that
CC       controls motility and adhesion in bacterial cells; it was first
CC       identified as having a positive allosteric effect on EC 2.4.1.12.
