//
ID   2.8.1.5
DE   Thiosulfate--dithiol sulfurtransferase.
AN   Thiosulfate reductase.
CA   Thiosulfate + dithioerythritol = sulfite + 4,5-cis-dihydroxy-1,2-
CA   dithiacyclohexane + sulfide.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Chlorella shows very little activity toward
CC       monothiols such as glutathione and cysteine (cf. EC 2.8.1.3).
CC   -!- The enzyme probably transfers the sulfur atom onto one thiol group to
CC       form -S-S(-), and sulfide is spontaneously expelled from this by
CC       reaction with the other thiol group.
CC   -!- May be identical with EC 2.8.1.1.
//
ID   2.8.1.6
DE   Biotin synthase.
CA   Dethiobiotin + sulfur-(sulfur carrier) + 2 S-adenosyl-L-methionine +
CA   2 reduced [2Fe-2S] ferredoxin = biotin + (sulfur carrier) +
CA   2 L-methionine + 2 5'-deoxyadenosine + 2 oxidized [2Fe-2S] ferredoxin.
CF   Iron-sulfur.
CC   -!- The enzyme binds a [4Fe-4S] and a [2Fe-2S] cluster.
CC   -!- In every reaction cycle, the enzyme consumes two molecules of AdoMet,
CC       each producing 5'-deoxyadenosine and a putative dethiobiotinyl carbon
CC       radical.
CC   -!- Reaction with another equivalent of AdoMet results in abstraction of
CC       the C6 methylene pro-S hydrogen atom from 9-mercaptodethiobiotin,
CC       and the resulting carbon radical is quenched via formation of an
CC       intramolecular C-S bond, thus closing the biotin thiophane ring.
CC   -!- The sulfur donor is believed to be the [2Fe-2S] cluster, which is
CC       sacrificed in the process, so that in vitro the reaction is a single
CC       turnover.
CC   -!- In vivo, the [2Fe-2S] cluster can be reassembled by the Isc or Suf
CC       iron-sulfur cluster assembly systems, to allow further catalysis.
