//
ID   2.8.1.8
DE   Lipoyl synthase.
AN   Lipoate synthase.
CA   Protein N(6)-(octanoyl)lysine + an [Fe-S] cluster scaffold protein
CA   carrying a [4Fe-4S](2+) cluster + 2 S-adenosyl-L-methionine + 2 oxidized
CA   [2Fe-2S] ferredoxin + 6 H(+) = protein N(6)-(dihydrolipoyl)lysine +
CA   an [Fe-S] cluster scaffold protein + 2 sulfide + 4 Fe(3+) +
CA   2 L-methionine + 2 5'-deoxyadenosine + 2 reduced [2Fe-2S] ferredoxin.
CF   Iron-sulfur.
CC   -!- This enzyme catalyzes the final step in the de-novo biosynthesis of
CC       the lipoyl cofactor, the attachment of two sulfhydryl groups to C(6)
CC       and C(8) of a pendant octanoyl chain.
CC   -!- It is a member of the 'AdoMet radical' (radical SAM) family,
CC       all members of which produce the 5'-deoxyadenosin-5'-yl radical and
CC       methionine from AdoMet (S-adenosylmethionine) by the addition of an
CC       electron from an iron-sulfur center.
CC   -!- The enzyme contains two [4Fe-4S] clusters.
CC   -!- The first cluster produces the radicals, which are converted into
CC       5'-deoxyadenosine when they abstract hydrogen atoms from C(6) and
CC       C(8), respectively, leaving reactive radicals at these positions that
CC       interact with sulfur atoms within the second (auxiliary) cluster.
CC   -!- Having donated two sulfur atoms, the auxiliary cluster is degraded
CC       during catalysis, but is regenerated immediately by the transfer of a
CC       new cluster from iron-sulfur cluster carrier proteins.
CC   -!- Lipoylation is essential for the function of several key enzymes
CC       involved in oxidative metabolism, as it converts apoprotein into the
CC       biologically active holoprotein.
CC   -!- Examples of such lipoylated proteins include pyruvate dehydrogenase
CC       (E(2) domain), 2-oxoglutarate dehydrogenase (E(2) domain),
CC       the branched-chain 2-oxoacid dehydrogenases and the glycine cleavage
CC       system (H protein).
CC   -!- An alternative lipoylation pathway involves EC 6.3.1.20, which can
CC       lipoylate apoproteins using exogenous lipoic acid (or its analogs).
