ID   2.8.3.22
DE   Succinyl-CoA--L-malate CoA-transferase.
CA   (1) Succinyl-CoA + (S)-malate = succinate + (S)-malyl-CoA.
CA   (2) Succinyl-CoA + (S)-citramalate = succinate + (S)-citramalyl-CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, purified from the bacterium Chloroflexus aurantiacus,
CC       can also accept itaconate as acceptor, with lower efficiency.
CC   -!- It is part of the 3-hydroxypropanoate cycle for carbon assimilation.
DR   A9WC40, SMTA_CHLAA ;  Q1KLK1, SMTA_CHLAU ;  A9WC39, SMTB_CHLAA ;
DR   Q1KLK0, SMTB_CHLAU ;
//
ID   2.8.3.23
DE   Caffeate CoA-transferase.
CA   3-(3,4-dihydroxyphenyl)propanoyl-CoA + (2E)-3-(3,4-dihydroxyphenyl)prop-
CA   2-enoate = 3-(3,4-dihydroxyphenyl)propanoate + (2E)-3-(3,4-
CA   dihydroxyphenyl)prop-2-enoyl-CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the bacterium Acetobacterium woodii,
CC       catalyzes an energy-saving CoA loop for caffeate activation.
CC   -!- In addition to caffeate, the enzyme can utilize 4-coumarate or
CC       ferulate as CoA acceptor.
DR   F1CYZ5, CARA_ACEWO ;
//
ID   2.8.3.24
DE   (R)-2-hydroxy-4-methylpentanoate CoA-transferase.
CA   4-methylpentanoyl-CoA + (R)-2-hydroxy-4-methylpentanoate =
CA   4-methylpentanoate + (R)-2-hydroxy-4-methylpentanoyl-CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Peptoclostridium
CC       difficile, participates in an L-leucine fermentation pathway.
CC   -!- The reaction proceeds via formation of a covalent anhydride
CC       intermediate between a conserved aspartate residue and the acyl group
CC       of the CoA thioester substrate.
//
ID   2.8.3.25
DE   Bile acid CoA-transferase.
AN   Bile acid coenzyme A transferase.
CA   (1) Lithocholoyl-CoA + cholate = lithocholate + choloyl-CoA.
CA   (2) Deoxycholoyl-CoA + cholate = deoxycholate + choloyl-CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the gut bacterium Clostridium
CC       scindens, catalyzes the last step in bile acid 7-alpha-
CC       dehydroxylation, the removal of the CoA moiety from the products.
CC   -!- By using a transferase rather than hydrolase, the bacteria conserve
CC       the thioester bond energy, saving ATP molecules.
CC   -!- The enzyme has a broad substrate specificity and can use multiple
CC       acceptors, including allocholate, ursodeoxycholate, and beta-
CC       muricholate.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.1.2.26.
DR   P19413, BAIF_CLOSV ;
//
ID   2.8.4.1
DE   Coenzyme-B sulfoethylthiotransferase.
AN   Methyl coenzyme M reductase.
AN   Methyl-CoM reductase.
CA   Methyl-CoM + CoB = CoM-S-S-CoB + methane.
CF   Coenzyme F430.
CC   -!- This enzyme catalyzes the final step in methanogenesis,
CC       the biological production of methane.
CC   -!- This important anaerobic process is carried out only by methanogenic
CC       archaea.
CC   -!- The enzyme can also function in reverse, for anaerobic oxidation of
CC       methane.
CC   -!- The enzyme requires the hydroporphinoid nickel complex coenzyme
CC       F(430).
CC   -!- Highly specific for coenzyme B with a heptanoyl chain; ethyl CoM and
CC       difluoromethyl CoM are poor substrates.
CC   -!- The sulfide sulfur can be replaced by selenium but not by oxygen.
DR   P07962, MCRA_METBF ;  P12971, MCRA_METFE ;  Q58256, MCRA_METJA ;
