ID   2.8.4.2
DE   Arsenate-mycothiol transferase.
CA   Arsenate + mycothiol = arseno-mycothiol + H(2)O.
CC   -!- Reduction of arsenate is part of a defense mechanism of the cell
CC       against toxic arsenate.
CC   -!- The product arseno-mycothiol is reduced by EC 1.20.4.3 (mycoredoxin)
CC       to arsenite and mycothiol-mycoredoxin disulfide.
CC   -!- Finally, a second mycothiol recycles mycoredoxin and forms
CC       mycothione.
DR   P0DKS6, ARSC1_CORGK;  P0DKS5, ARSC1_CORGL;  P0DKS8, ARSC2_CORGK;
DR   P0DKS7, ARSC2_CORGL;
//
ID   2.8.4.3
DE   tRNA-2-methylthio-N(6)-dimethylallyladenosine synthase.
AN   2-methylthio-N-6-isopentenyl adenosine synthase.
AN   tRNA-i6A37 methylthiotransferase.
CA   N(6)-dimethylallyladenine(37) in tRNA + sulfur-(sulfur carrier) +
CA   2 S-adenosyl-L-methionine + reduced electron acceptor = 2-methylthio-
CA   N(6)-dimethylallyladenine(37) in tRNA + S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine +
CA   (sulfur carrier) + L-methionine + 5'-deoxyadenosine + electron acceptor.
CF   Iron-sulfur.
CC   -!- This bacterial enzyme binds two [4Fe-4S] clusters as well as the
CC       transferred sulfur.
CC   -!- The enzyme is a member of the superfamily of S-adenosyl-L-methionine-
CC       dependent radical (radical AdoMet) enzymes.
CC   -!- The sulfur donor is believed to be one of the [4Fe-4S] clusters,
CC       which is sacrificed in the process, so that in vitro the reaction is
CC       a single turnover.
CC   -!- The identity of the electron donor is not known.
