DE   Poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) depolymerase.
AN   PHB depolymerase.
AN   Poly((R)-hydroxyalkanoic acid) depolymerase.
AN   Poly(3HB) depolymerase.
AN   Poly(HA(SCL)) depolymerase.
AN   Poly(HA) depolymerase.
CA   ((R)-3-hydroxybutanoate)(n) + H(2)O = ((R)-3-hydroxybutanoate)(n-1 to 5)
CA   + ((R)-3-hydroxybutanoate)(1-5).
CC   -!- Reaction also occurs with esters of other short-chain-length (C(1)-
CC       C(5)) hydroxyalkanoic acids (HA).
CC   -!- There are two types of polymers: native (intracellular) granules are
CC       amorphous and have an intact surface layer; denatured (extracellular)
CC       granules either have no surface layer or a damaged surface layer and
CC       are partially crystalline.
DR   P12625, PHB_RALPI  ;
//
ID   3.1.1.76
DE   Poly(3-hydroxyoctanoate) depolymerase.
AN   PHA depolymerase.
AN   PHO depolymerase.
AN   Poly((R)-3-hydroxyoctanoate) hydrolase.
AN   Poly((R)-hydroxyalkanoic acid) depolymerase.
AN   Poly(3HO) depolymerase.
AN   Poly(HA(MCL)) depolymerase.
AN   Poly(HA) depolymerase.
CA   Hydrolyzes the polyester poly(oxycarbonyl((R)-2-pentylethylene)) to
CA   oligomers.
CC   -!- The main product after prolonged incubation is the dimer.
CC   -!- Besides hydrolyzing polymers of 3-hydroxyoctanoic acid, the enzyme
CC       also hydrolyzes other polymers derived from medium-chain-length
CC       (C(6)-C(12)) hydroxyalkanoic acids and copolymers of mixtures of
CC       these.
CC   -!- It also hydrolyzes p-nitrophenyl esters of fatty acids.
CC   -!- Polymers of short-chain-length hydroxyalkanoic acids such as
CC       poly((R)-3-hydroxybutanoic acid) and poly((R)-3-hydroxypentanoic
CC       acid) are not hydrolyzed.
DR   Q51718, PHAZ_PSEFL ;
//
ID   3.1.1.77
DE   Acyloxyacyl hydrolase.
CA   3-(acyloxy)acyl group of bacterial toxin = 3-hydroxyacyl group of
CA   bacterial toxin + a fatty acid.
CC   -!- The substrate is lipid A on the reducing end of the toxic
CC       lipopolysaccharide (LPS) of Salmonella typhimurium and related
CC       organisms.
CC   -!- It consists of diglucosamine, beta-D-GlcN-(1->6)-D-GlcN, attached by
CC       glycosylation on O-6 of its non-reducing residue, phosphorylated on
CC       O-4 of this residue and on O-1 of its potentially reducing residue.
CC   -!- Both residues carry 3-(acyloxy)acyl groups on N-2 and O-3.
CC   -!- The enzyme from human leukocytes detoxifies the lipid by hydrolyzing
CC       the secondary acyl groups from O-3 of the 3-hydroxyacyl groups on the
CC       disaccharide (LPS).
CC   -!- It also possesses a wide range of phospholipase and acyltransferase
CC       activities (e.g. EC 3.1.1.4, EC 3.1.1.5, EC 3.1.1.32 and
CC       EC 3.1.1.52), hydrolyzing diacylglycerol and phosphatidyl compounds,
CC       but not triacylglycerols.
CC   -!- It has a preference for saturated C(12)-C(16) acyl groups.
DR   P28039, AOAH_HUMAN ;  O35298, AOAH_MOUSE ;  O18823, AOAH_RABIT ;
DR   Q7WD07, PAGL_BORBR ;  Q9HVD1, PAGL_PSEAE ;
//
ID   3.1.1.78
DE   Polyneuridine-aldehyde esterase.
AN   PNAE.
AN   Polyneuridine aldehyde esterase.
CA   Polyneuridine aldehyde + H(2)O = 16-epivellosimine + CO(2) + methanol.
CC   -!- Following hydrolysis of this indole alkaloid ester, the carboxylic
CC       acid decarboxylates spontaneously giving the sarpagan skeleton.
CC   -!- Also acts on akuammidine aldehyde (the 16-epimer of polyneuridine
CC       aldehyde).
DR   Q9SE93, PNAE_RAUSE ;
//
ID   3.1.1.79
DE   Hormone-sensitive lipase.
AN   HSL.
CA   (1) Diacylglycerol + H(2)O = monoacylglycerol + a carboxylate.
CA   (2) Triacylglycerol + H(2)O = diacylglycerol + a carboxylate.
CA   (3) Monoacylglycerol + H(2)O = glycerol + a carboxylate.
CC   -!- This enzyme is a serine hydrolase.
CC   -!- Compared with other lipases, hormone-sensitive lipase has a uniquely
CC       broad substrate specificity.
CC   -!- It hydrolyzes all acylglycerols (triacylglycerol, diacylglycerol and
CC       monoacylglycerol) as well as cholesteryl esters, steroid fatty acid
CC       esters, retinyl esters and p-nitrophenyl esters.
CC   -!- It exhibits a preference for the 1- or 3-ester bond of its
CC       acylglycerol substrate compared with the 2-ester bond.
CC   -!- The enzyme shows little preference for the fatty acids in the
CC       triacylglycerol, although there is some increase in activity with
CC       decreasing chain length.
CC   -!- The enzyme activity is increased in response to hormones that elevate
CC       intracellular levels of cAMP.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00903;
DR   P16386, LIPS_BOVIN ;  Q05469, LIPS_HUMAN ;  Q9R101, LIPS_ICTTR ;
DR   P54310, LIPS_MOUSE ;  Q68J42, LIPS_PIG   ;  P15304, LIPS_RAT   ;
//
ID   3.1.1.80
DE   Acetylajmaline esterase.
AN   2-beta-(R)-17-O-acetylajmalan:acetylesterase.
AN   AAE.
AN   Acetylajmalan esterase.
CA   (1) 17-O-acetylajmaline + H(2)O = ajmaline + acetate.
CA   (2) 17-O-acetylnorajmaline + H(2)O = norajmaline + acetate.
CC   -!- Responsible for the last stages in the biosynthesis of the indole
CC       alkaloid ajmaline.
CC   -!- Highly specific for the substrates 17-O-acetylajmaline and 17-O-
CC       acetylnorajmaline as the structurally related acetylated alkaloids
CC       vinorine, vomilenine, 1,2-dihydrovomilenine and 1,2-
CC       dihydroraucaffricine cannot act as substrates.
DR   Q3MKY2, AAE_RAUSE  ;  P86830, AAE_RAUVE  ;
//
ID   3.1.1.81
DE   Quorum-quenching N-acyl-homoserine lactonase.
AN   Acyl homoserine degrading enzyme.
AN   Acyl-homoserine-lactone acylase.
AN   AHL lactonase.
AN   AHL-degrading enzyme.
AN   AHL-inactivating enzyme.
AN   AHLase.
AN   N-acyl homoserine lactonase.
AN   N-acyl homoserine lactone hydrolase.
AN   N-acyl-homoserine lactone lactonase.
AN   N-acyl-L-homoserine lactone hydrolase.
AN   Quorum-quenching lactonase.
AN   Quorum-quenching N-acyl homoserine lactone hydrolase.
CA   An N-acyl-L-homoserine lactone + H(2)O = an N-acyl-L-homoserine.
CC   -!- Acyl-homoserine lactones (AHLs) are produced by a number of bacterial
CC       species and are used by them to regulate the expression of virulence
CC       genes in a process known as quorum-sensing.
CC   -!- Each bacterial cell has a basal level of AHL and, once the population
CC       density reaches a critical level, it triggers AHL-signaling which,
CC       in turn, initiates the expression of particular virulence genes.
CC   -!- Plants or animals capable of degrading AHLs would have a therapeutic
CC       advantage in avoiding bacterial infection as they could prevent AHL-
CC       signaling and the expression of virulence genes in quorum-sensing
CC       bacteria.
CC   -!- N-(3-oxohexanoyl)-L-homoserine lactone, N-(3-oxododecanoyl)-L-
CC       homoserine lactone, N-butanoyl-L-homoserine lactone and
CC       N-(3-oxooctanoyl)-L-homoserine lactone can act as substrates.
