ID   3.1.1.82
DE   Pheophorbidase.
AN   Phedase.
AN   PPD.
CA   Pheophorbide a + H(2)O = pyropheophorbide a + methanol + CO(2).
CC   -!- Forms part of the chlorophyll degradation pathway, and is found in
CC       higher plants and in algae.
CC   -!- In higher plants it participates in de-greening processes such as
CC       fruit ripening, leaf senescence, and flowering.
CC   -!- Exists in two forms: type 1 is induced by senescence whereas type 2
CC       is constitutively expressed.
CC   -!- Highly specific for pheophorbide as substrate (with a preference for
CC       pheophorbide a over pheophorbide b) as other chlorophyll derivatives
CC       such as protochlorophyllide a, pheophytin a and c, chlorophyll a and
CC       b, and chlorophyllide a cannot act as substrates.
CC   -!- Another enzyme, called pheophorbide demethoxycarbonylase (PDC),
CC       produces pyropheophorbide a from pheophorbide a without forming an
CC       intermediate although the precise reaction is not yet known.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00110;
DR   O23512, PPD_ARATH  ;  Q2V0W1, PPD_RAPSA  ;
//
ID   3.1.1.83
DE   Monoterpene epsilon-lactone hydrolase.
AN   MLH.
CA   (1) 4-isopropenyl-7-methyloxepan-2-one + H(2)O = 6-hydroxy-3-
CA   isopropenylheptanoate.
CA   (2) 7-isopropyl-4-methyloxepan-2-one + H(2)O = 6-hydroxy-3,7-
CA   dimethyloctanoate.
CC   -!- Catalyzes the ring opening of epsilon-lactones which are formed
CC       during degradation of dihydrocarveol by the Gram-positive bacterium
CC       Rhodococcus erythropolis DCL14.
CC   -!- Also acts on ethyl caproate, indicating that it is an esterase with a
CC       preference for lactones (internal cyclic esters).
CC   -!- Not stereoselective.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00903;
DR   Q9EX73, MLHB_RHOER ;
//
ID   3.1.1.84
DE   Cocaine esterase.
AN   Human carboxylesterase 2.
CA   Cocaine + H(2)O = ecgonine methyl ester + benzoate.
CC   -!- Rhodococcus sp. strain MB1 and Pseudomonas maltophilia strain MB11L
CC       can utilize cocaine as sole source of carbon and energy.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00112;
DR   Q9L9D7, COCE_RHOSM ;  O00748, EST2_HUMAN ;
//
ID   3.1.1.85
DE   Pimeloyl-[acyl-carrier protein] methyl ester esterase.
CA   Pimeloyl-[acyl-carrier protein] methyl ester + H(2)O = pimeloyl-[acyl-
CA   carrier protein] + methanol.
CC   -!- Involved in biotin biosynthesis in Gram-negative bacteria.
CC   -!- The enzyme exhibits carboxylesterase activity, particularly toward
CC       substrates with short acyl chains.
CC   -!- Even though the enzyme can interact with coenzyme A thioesters,
CC       the in vivo role of the enzyme is to hydrolyze the methyl ester of
CC       pimeloyl-[acyl carrier protein], terminating the part of the biotin
CC       biosynthesis pathway that is catalyzed by the fatty acid elongation
CC       enzymes.
