CC       glucosamine 3-O-sulfate; the mammalian enzyme acts only on the
CC       disulfated residue.
//
ID   3.1.6.16
DE   Monomethyl-sulfatase.
CA   Monomethyl sulfate + H(2)O = methanol + sulfate.
CC   -!- Highly specific; does not act on monoethyl sulfate, monoisopropyl
CC       sulfate or monododecyl sulfate.
//
ID   3.1.6.17
DE   D-lactate-2-sulfatase.
CA   (R)-2-O-sulfolactate + H(2)O = (R)-lactate + sulfate.
CC   -!- Highly specific.
//
ID   3.1.6.18
DE   Glucuronate-2-sulfatase.
AN   Chondro-2-sulfatase.
CA   Hydrolysis of the 2-sulfate groups of the 2-O-sulfo-D-glucuronate
CA   residues of chondroitin sulfate, heparin and heparitin sulfate.
CC   -!- Does not act on iduronate 2-sulfate residues (cf. EC 3.1.6.13).
//
ID   3.1.6.19
DE   (R)-specific secondary-alkylsulfatase.
AN   (R)-specific sec-alkylsulfatase.
AN   Sec-alkylsulfatase.
CA   An (R)-secondary-alkyl sulfate + H(2)O = an (S)-secondary-alcohol +
CA   sulfate.
CF   Zn(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme from Rhodococcus ruber is involved in the biodegradation
CC       of alkyl sulfate esters used as detergents and released into the
CC       environment.
CC   -!- The preferred substrates are linear secondary-alkyl sulfate esters,
CC       particularly octan-2-yl, octan-3-yl, and octan-4-yl sulfates.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Pseudomonas sp. DSM6611 utilizes a range of
CC       secondary-alkyl sulfate esters bearing aromatic, olefinic and
CC       acetylenic moieties.
CC   -!- Perfect enantioselectivities are obtained for substrates bearing
CC       groups of different size adjacent to the sulfate moiety.
CC   -!- The enzymatic hydrolysis proceeds through inversion of the
CC       configuration at the stereogenic carbon atom.
//
ID   3.1.6.20
DE   S-sulfosulfanyl-L-cysteine sulfohydrolase.
CA   (1) [SoxY protein]-S-sulfosulfanyl-L-cysteine + H(2)O = [SoxY protein]-S-
CA   sulfanyl-L-cysteine + sulfate.
CA   (2) [SoxY protein]-S-(2-sulfodisulfanyl)-L-cysteine + H(2)O = [SoxY
CA   protein]-S-disulfanyl-L-cysteine + sulfate.
CF   Mn(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme is part of the Sox enzyme system, which participates in a
CC       bacterial thiosulfate oxidation pathway that produces sulfate.
CC   -!- It catalyzes two reactions in the pathway.
CC   -!- In both cases the enzyme hydrolyzes a sulfonate moiety that is bound
CC       (either directly or via a sulfane) to a cysteine residue of a SoxY
CC       protein, releasing sulfate.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Paracoccus pantotrophus contains a pyroglutamate
CC       (cycloglutamate) at its N-terminus.
//
ID   3.1.7.1
DE   Prenyl-diphosphatase.
AN   Prenyl-pyrophosphatase.
CA   Prenyl diphosphate + H(2)O = prenol + diphosphate.
CC   -!- Farnesyl diphosphate is the best substrate so far tested.
//
ID   3.1.7.2
DE   Guanosine-3',5'-bis(diphosphate) 3'-diphosphatase.
AN   (ppGpp)ase.
AN   Guanosine-3',5'-bis(diphosphate) 3'-pyrophosphohydrolase.
AN   Penta-phosphate guanosine-3'-diphosphohydrolase.
AN   Penta-phosphate guanosine-3'-pyrophosphohydrolase.
CA   Guanosine 3',5'-bis(diphosphate) + H(2)O = guanosine 5'-diphosphate +
CA   diphosphate.
