//
ID   3.2.2.24
DE   ADP-ribosyl-[dinitrogen reductase] hydrolase.
AN   ADP-ribosyl glycohydrolase.
AN   Dinitrogenase reductase activating glycohydrolase.
CA   [Dinitrogen reductase]-N(omega)-alpha-(ADP-D-ribosyl)-L-arginine = ADP-D-
CA   ribose + [dinitrogen reductase]-L-arginine.
CC   -!- The enzyme restores the activity of EC 1.18.6.1 by catalyzing the
CC       removal of ADP-ribose from an arginine residue of the dinitrogenase
CC       reductase component of nitrogenase.
CC   -!- This activity occurs only when the nitrogenase product, ammonium,
CC       is not available.
CC   -!- The combined activity of this enzyme and EC 2.4.2.37 controls the
CC       level of activity of nitrogenase.
DR   P14300, DRAG_RHORU ;
//
ID   3.2.2.25
DE   N-methyl nucleosidase.
AN   7-methylxanthosine nucleosidase.
AN   Methylpurine nucleosidase.
AN   N-meNase.
AN   N-methyl nucleoside hydrolase.
CA   7-methylxanthosine + H(2)O = 7-methylxanthine + D-ribose.
CC   -!- Preferentially hydrolyzes 3- and 7-methylpurine nucleosides, such as
CC       3-methylxanthosine, 3-methyladenosine and 7-methylguanosine.
CC   -!- Hydrolysis of 7-methylxanthosine to form 7-methylxanthine is the
CC       second step in the caffeine biosynthesis pathway.
//
ID   3.2.2.26
DE   Futalosine hydrolase.
AN   Futalosine nucleosidase.
CA   Futalosine + H(2)O = dehypoxanthine futalosine + hypoxanthine.
CC   -!- This enzyme, which is specific for futalosine, catalyzes the second
CC       step of a novel menaquinone biosynthetic pathway that is found in
CC       some prokaryotes.
DR   A0LR22, MQNB_ACIC1 ;  Q9KXN0, MQNB_STRCO ;  Q5SKT7, MQNB_THET8 ;
//
ID   3.2.2.27
DE   Uracil-DNA glycosylase.
AN   Uracil DNA glycohydrolase.
AN   Uracil-DNA N-glycosylase.
CA   Hydrolyzes single-stranded DNA or mismatched double-stranded DNA and
CA   polynucleotides, releasing free uracil.
CC   -!- Uracil-DNA glycosylases are widespread enzymes that are found in all
CC       living organisms.
CC   -!- Uracil-DNA glycosylase (EC 3.2.2.27) and double-stranded uracil-DNA
CC       glycosylase (EC 3.2.2.28) form a central part of the DNA-repair
CC       machinery since they initiate the DNA base-excision repair pathway by
CC       hydrolyzing the N-glycosidic bond between uracil and the deoxyribose
CC       sugar thereby catalyzing the removal of mis-incorporated uracil from
CC       DNA.
