ID   3.5.1.53
DE   N-carbamoylputrescine amidase.
CA   N-carbamoylputrescine + H(2)O = putrescine + CO(2) + NH(3).
DR   Q93XI4, AGUB_ORYSJ ;  Q9XGI9, AGUB_SOLLC ;  Q3HVN1, AGUB_SOLTU ;
DR   Q8VYF5, NILP1_ARATH;
//
ID   3.5.1.54
DE   Allophanate hydrolase.
CA   Urea-1-carboxylate + H(2)O = 2 CO(2) + 2 NH(3).
CC   -!- Along with EC 3.5.2.15 and EC 3.5.1.84, forms part of the cyanuric-
CC       acid metabolism pathway, which degrades s-triazide herbicides, such
CC       as atrazine (2-chloro-4-(ethylamino)-6-(isopropylamino)-1,3,5-
CC       triazine), in bacteria.
CC   -!- The Saccharomyces cerevisiae enzyme (but not that from green algae)
CC       also catalyzes the reaction of EC 6.3.4.6, thus bringing about the
CC       hydrolysis of urea to CO(2) and NH(3) in the presence of ATP and
CC       bicarbonate.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Pseudomonas sp. strain ADP has a narrow substrate
CC       specificity, being unable to use the structurally analogous compounds
CC       urea, hydroxyurea or methylcarbamate as substrate.
DR   Q936X2, ATZF_PSESD ;  A5H0J2, DUR1_LACKL ;  P32528, DUR1_YEAST ;
//
ID   3.5.1.55
DE   Long-chain-fatty-acyl-glutamate deacylase.
AN   Long-chain aminoacylase.
CA   N-long-chain-fatty-acyl-L-glutamate + H(2)O = a long-chain carboxylate +
CA   L-glutamate.
CC   -!- Does not act on acyl derivatives of other amino acids.
CC   -!- Optimum chain length of acyl residue is 12 to 16.
//
ID   3.5.1.56
DE   N,N-dimethylformamidase.
CA   N,N-dimethylformamide + H(2)O = dimethylamine + formate.
CF   Fe cation.
CC   -!- Also acts on N-ethylformamide and N-methylformamide and, more slowly,
CC       on N,N-diethylformamide, N,N-dimethylacetamide and unsubstituted acyl
CC       amides.
DR   Q9LCC1, DMFAA_ALCSP;  C9DQ22, DMFAA_PARAH;  Q9LCC0, DMFAB_ALCSP;
DR   C9DQ21, DMFAB_PARAH;
//
ID   3.5.1.57
DE   Tryptophanamidase.
CA   L-tryptophanamide + H(2)O = L-tryptophan + NH(3).
CF   Mn(2+).
CC   -!- Also acts on N-ethylformamide and L-tyrosinamide, and on some
CC       tryptophan dipeptides.
//
ID   3.5.1.58
DE   N-benzyloxycarbonylglycine hydrolase.
CA   N-benzyloxycarbonylglycine + H(2)O = benzyl alcohol + CO(2) + glycine.
CF   Cobalt cation; Zn(2+).
CC   -!- Also acts, more slowly, on N-benzyloxycarbonylalanine, but not on the
CC       corresponding derivatives of other amino acids or on
CC       N-benzyloxycarbonylpeptides (cf. EC 3.5.1.64).
//
ID   3.5.1.59
DE   N-carbamoylsarcosine amidase.
AN   CSHase.
AN   N-carbamoylsarcosine amidohydrolase.
CA   N-carbamoylsarcosine + H(2)O = sarcosine + CO(2) + NH(3).
DR   P32400, CSH_ARTSP  ;
//
ID   3.5.1.60
DE   N-(long-chain-acyl)ethanolamine deacylase.
AN   N-acylethanolamine amidohydrolase.
CA   N-(long-chain-acyl)ethanolamine + H(2)O = a long-chain carboxylate +
CA   ethanolamine.
CC   -!- Does not act on N-acylsphingosine or N,O-diacylethanolamine.
//
ID   3.5.1.61
DE   Mimosinase.
AN   Mimosine amidohydrolase.
CA   (S)-2-amino-3-(3-hydroxy-4-oxo-4H-pyridin-1-yl)propanoate + H(2)O =
CA   3-hydroxy-4H-pyrid-4-one + L-serine.
CC   -!- An enzyme from Leucaena leucocephala leaf, which also contains the
CC       toxic amino acid, mimosine.
//
ID   3.5.1.62
DE   Acetylputrescine deacetylase.
CA   N-acetylputrescine + H(2)O = acetate + putrescine.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Micrococcus luteus also acts on N(8)-acetylspermidine
CC       and acetylcadaverine, but more slowly.
DR   Q9I3T5, APAH1_PSEAE;  Q9I6H0, APAH2_PSEAE;  Q3JUN4, APAHL_BURP1;
DR   Q48935, APAH_MYCRA ;
//
ID   3.5.1.63
DE   4-acetamidobutyrate deacetylase.
CA   4-acetamidobutanoate + H(2)O = acetate + 4-aminobutanoate.
CC   -!- Also acts on N-acetyl-beta-alanine and 5-acetamidopentanoate.
//
ID   3.5.1.64
DE   N(alpha)-benzyloxycarbonylleucine hydrolase.
CA   N(alpha)-benzyloxycarbonyl-L-leucine + H(2)O = benzyl alcohol + CO(2) +
CA   L-leucine.
CC   -!- Also acts on N(alpha)-t-butoxycarbonyl-L-leucine, and, more slowly,
CC       on the corresponding derivatives of L-aspartate, L-methionine,
CC       L-glutamate and L-alanine (cf. EC 3.5.1.58).
//
ID   3.5.1.65
DE   Theanine hydrolase.
CA   N(5)-ethyl-L-glutamine + H(2)O = L-glutamate + ethylamine.
CC   -!- Also acts on other N-alkyl-L-glutamines.
//
ID   3.5.1.66
DE   2-(hydroxymethyl)-3-(acetamidomethylene)succinate hydrolase.
CA   2-(hydroxymethyl)-3-(acetamidomethylene)succinate + 2 H(2)O = acetate +
CA   2-(hydroxymethyl)-4-oxobutanoate + NH(3) + CO(2).
CC   -!- Involved in the degradation of pyridoxin by Pseudomonas and
CC       Arthrobacter.
//
ID   3.5.1.67
DE   4-methyleneglutaminase.
AN   4-methyleneglutamine deamidase.
CA   4-methylene-L-glutamine + H(2)O = 4-methylene-L-glutamate + NH(3).
//
ID   3.5.1.68
DE   N-formylglutamate deformylase.
AN   Beta-citryl-L-glutamate hydrolase.
CA   N-formyl-L-glutamate + H(2)O = formate + L-glutamate.
CC   -!- The animal enzyme also acts on beta-citryl-L-glutamate and beta-
CC       citryl-L-glutamine.
//
ID   3.5.1.69
DE   Glycosphingolipid deacylase.
AN   Glycosphingolipid ceramide deacylase.
CA   Hydrolysis of gangliosides and neutral glycosphingolipids, releasing
CA   fatty acids to form the lyso-derivatives.
CC   -!- Does not act on sphingolipids such as ceramide.
CC   -!- Not identical with EC 3.5.1.23.
//
ID   3.5.1.70
DE   Aculeacin-A deacylase.
CA   Hydrolysis of the amide bond in aculeacin A and related neutral
CA   lipopeptide antibiotics, releasing the long-chain fatty acid side-chain.
//
ID   3.5.1.71
DE   N-feruloylglycine deacylase.
CA   N-feruloylglycine + H(2)O = ferulate + glycine.
CC   -!- Hydrolyzes a range of L-amino acids from the cinnamoyl and
CC       substituted cinnamoyl series.
CC   -!- Not identical with EC 3.5.1.14.
//
ID   3.5.1.72
DE   D-benzoylarginine-4-nitroanilide amidase.
CA   N-benzoyl-D-arginine-4-nitroanilide + H(2)O = N-benzoyl-D-arginine +
CA   4-nitroaniline.
//
ID   3.5.1.73
DE   Carnitinamidase.
CA   L-carnitinamide + H(2)O = L-carnitine + NH(3).
CC   -!- Does not act on D-carnitinamide.
//
ID   3.5.1.74
DE   Chenodeoxycholoyltaurine hydrolase.
CA   Chenodeoxycholoyltaurine + H(2)O = chenodeoxycholate + taurine.
CC   -!- Some other taurine conjugates are hydrolyzed, but not glycine
CC       conjugates of bile acids.
//
ID   3.5.1.75
DE   Urethanase.
AN   Urethane hydrolase.
CA   Urethane + H(2)O = ethanol + CO(2) + NH(3).
//
ID   3.5.1.76
DE   Arylalkyl acylamidase.
AN   Aralkyl acylamidase.
CA   N-acetylarylalkylamine + H(2)O = arylalkylamine + acetate.
CC   -!- Strict specificity for N-acetyl arylalkylamines, including N-acetyl-
CC       2-phenylethylamine, N-acetyl-3-phenylpropylamine, N-acetyldopamine,
CC       N-acetyl-serotonin and melatonin.
CC   -!- It also accepts arylalkyl acetates but not acetanilide derivatives,
CC       which are common substrates of EC 3.5.1.13.
DR   P35902, ARYA_PSEPU ;
//
ID   3.5.1.77
DE   N-carbamoyl-D-amino-acid hydrolase.
AN   D-N-carbamoylase.
AN   N-carbamoyl-D-amino acid hydrolase.
AN   N-carbamoylase.
CA   N-carbamoyl-D-amino acid + H(2)O = D-amino acid + NH(3) + CO(2).
CC   -!- This enzyme, along with EC 3.5.1.87, EC 5.1.99.5 and EC 3.5.2.2,
CC       forms part of the reaction cascade known as the 'hydantoinase
CC       process', which allows the total conversion of D,L-5-monosubstituted
CC       hydantoins into optically pure D- or L-amino acids.
CC   -!- Has strict stereospecificity for N-carbamoyl-D-amino acids and does
CC       not act upon the corresponding L-amino acids or N-formyl amino acids,
CC       N-carbamoyl-sarcosine, -citrulline, -allantoin and -ureidopropanoate,
CC       which are substrates for other amidohydrolases.
DR   P60327, DCAS_AGRSK ;  Q5S260, DCAS_ENSAD ;  Q44185, DCAS_RHIRD ;
//
ID   3.5.1.78
DE   Glutathionylspermidine amidase.
AN   Glutathionylspermidine amidohydrolase (spermidine-forming).
AN   GSP amidase.
CA   Glutathionylspermidine + H(2)O = glutathione + spermidine.
CC   -!- Transforms glutathionylspermidine into glutathione and spermidine.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Escherichia coli is bifunctional and also catalyzes
CC       the reaction of EC 6.3.1.8, resulting in a net hydrolysis of ATP.
DR   P0AES0, GSP_ECOLI  ;  P0AES1, GSP_SHIFL  ;
//
ID   3.5.1.79
DE   Phthalyl amidase.
AN   o-phthalyl amidase.
CA   A phthalylamide + H(2)O = phthalic acid + a substituted amine.
CC   -!- Phthalyl is used to mean 2-carboxybenzoyl.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Xanthobacter agilis hydrolyzes phthalylated amino
CC       acids, peptides, beta-lactams, aromatic and aliphatic amines.
CC   -!- The substituent on nitrogen may be an alkyl group, but may also be
CC       complex, giving an amino acid or peptide derivative.
CC   -!- Substitutions on the phthalyl ring include 6-F, 6-NH(2), 3-OH, and a
CC       nitrogen in the aromatic ring ortho to the carboxy group attached to
CC       the amine.
DR   P0C2Y0, PHAM_XANAG ;
