//
ID   3.5.1.84
DE   Biuret amidohydrolase.
CA   Biuret + H(2)O = urea-1-carboxylate + NH(3).
CC   -!- Along with EC 3.5.2.15 and EC 3.5.1.54, this enzyme forms part of the
CC       cyanuric-acid metabolism pathway, which degrades s-triazide
CC       herbicides, such as atrazine (2-chloro-4-(ethylamino)-6-
CC       (isopropylamino)-1,3,5-triazine), in bacteria.
CC   -!- Urea-1-carboxylate rather than urea (as was thought previously) is
CC       the 2-nitrogen intermediate in cyanuric-acid metabolism in bacteria.
CC   -!- The product, urea-1-carboxylate, can spontaneously decarboxylate
CC       under acidic conditions to form urea but, under physiological
CC       conditions, it can be converted into CO(2) and ammonia by the action
CC       of EC 3.5.1.54.
DR   Q936X3, ATZE_PSESD ;
//
ID   3.5.1.85
DE   (S)-N-acetyl-1-phenylethylamine hydrolase.
AN   (S)-N-acetyl-1-phenylethylamine amidohydrolase.
CA   N-acetylphenylethylamine + H(2)O = phenylethylamine + acetate.
CC   -!- Inhibited by phenylmethanesulfonyl fluoride.
CC   -!- Some related acetylated compounds are hydrolyzed with variable
CC       enantiomeric selectivities.
//
ID   3.5.1.86
DE   Mandelamide amidase.
AN   Mandelamide hydrolase.
CA   (R)-mandelamide + H(2)O = (R)-mandelate + NH(3).
