ID   3.5.1.123
DE   Gamma-glutamylanilide hydrolase.
CA   N(5)-phenyl-L-glutamine + H(2)O = L-glutamate + aniline.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Acinetobacter sp. YAA,
CC       catalyzes the opposite reaction from that cayalysed by EC 6.3.1.18,
CC       which is part of an aniline degradation pathway.
CC   -!- Its purpose is likely to maintain a low concentration of N(5)-phenyl-
CC       L-glutamine, which is potentially toxic.
//
ID   3.5.1.124
DE   Protein deglycase.
AN   Glyoxylase III.
CA   (1) An N(omega)-(1-hydroxy-2-oxopropyl)-[protein]-L-arginine + H(2)O =
CA   a [protein]-L-arginine + lactate.
CA   (2) An N(6)-(1-hydroxy-2-oxopropyl)-[protein]-L-lysine + H(2)O =
CA   a [protein]-L-lysine + lactate.
CA   (3) An S-(1-hydroxy-2-oxopropyl)-[protein]-L-cysteine + H(2)O =
CA   a [protein]-L-cysteine + lactate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, previously thought to be a glyoxalase, acts on glycated
CC       L-arginine, L-lysine, and L-cysteine residues within proteins that
CC       have been attacked and modified by glyoxal or 2-oxopropanal.
CC   -!- The attack forms hemithioacetal in the case of cysteines and
CC       aminocarbinols in the case of arginines and lysines.
CC   -!- The enzyme repairs the amino acids, releasing glycolate or lactate
CC       (70-80% (S)-lactate and 20-30% (R)-lactate), depending on whether the
CC       attacking agent was glyoxal or 2-oxopropanal, respectively.
