//
ID   3.6.5.1
DE   Heterotrimeric G-protein GTPase.
CA   GTP + H(2)O = GDP + phosphate.
CC   -!- This group comprises GTP-hydrolyzing systems, where GTP and GDP
CC       alternate in binding.
CC   -!- This group includes stimulatory and inhibitory G-proteins such as
CC       G(s), G(i), G(o) and G(olf), targeting adenylate cyclase and/or K(+)
CC       and Ca(2+) channels; G(q) stimulating phospholipase C; transducin
CC       activating cGMP phosphodiesterase; gustducin activating cAMP
CC       phosphodiesterase.
CC   -!- G(olf) is instrumental in odor perception, transducin in vision and
CC       gustducin in taste recognition.
CC   -!- At least 16 different alpha subunits (39-52 kDa), 5 beta subunits (36
CC       kDa) and 12 gamma subunits (6-9 kDa) are known.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.1.46.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC01002;
//
ID   3.6.5.2
DE   Small monomeric GTPase.
CA   GTP + H(2)O = GDP + phosphate.
CC   -!- A family of about 50 enzymes with a molecular mass of 21 kDa that are
CC       distantly related to the alpha-subunit of EC 3.6.1.46.
CC   -!- Involved in cell-growth regulation (Ras subfamily), membrane vesicle
CC       traffic and uncoating (Rab and ARF subfamilies), nuclear protein
CC       import (Ran subfamily) and organization of the cytoskeleton (Rho and
CC       Rac subfamilies).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.1.47.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00859;
DR   Q94CF0, EHD1_ARATH ;  B3LF48, EHD2_ARATH ;
//
ID   3.6.5.3
DE   Protein-synthesizing GTPase.
AN   Elongation factor (EF).
AN   Initiation factor (IF).
AN   Peptide-release factor (RF).
AN   Peptide-release or termination factor.
CA   GTP + H(2)O = GDP + phosphate.
CC   -!- This enzyme comprises a family of proteins involved in prokaryotic as
CC       well as eukaryotic protein synthesis.
CC   -!- In the initiation factor complex, it is IF-2b (98 kDa) that binds GTP
CC       and subsequently hydrolyzes it in prokaryotes.
CC   -!- In eukaryotes, it is eIF-2 (150 kDa) that binds GTP.
CC   -!- In the elongation phase, the GTP-hydrolyzing proteins are the EF-Tu
CC       polypeptide of the prokaryotic transfer factor (43 kDa),
CC       the eukaryotic elongation factor EF-1-alpha (53 kDa), the prokaryotic
CC       EF-G (77 kDa), the eukaryotic EF-2 (70-110 kDa) and the signal
CC       recognition particle that play a role in endoplasmic reticulum
CC       protein synthesis (325 kDa).
CC   -!- EF-Tu and EF-1-alpha catalyze binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the
CC       ribosomal A-site, while EF-G and EF-2 catalyze the translocation of
CC       peptidyl-tRNA from the A-site to the P-site.
CC   -!- GTPase activity is also involved in polypeptide release from the
CC       ribosome with the aid of the pRFs and eRFs.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.1.48.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00273;
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00905;
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00970;
DR   G0S8G9, IF2P_CHATD ;  Q54XP6, IF2P_DICDI ;  O60841, IF2P_HUMAN ;
DR   Q05D44, IF2P_MOUSE ;  Q5RDE1, IF2P_PONAB ;  B2GUV7, IF2P_RAT   ;
DR   Q10251, IF2P_SCHPO ;  P39730, IF2P_YEAST ;
//
ID   3.6.5.4
DE   Signal-recognition-particle GTPase.
CA   GTP + H(2)O = GDP + phosphate.
CC   -!- Activity is associated with the signal-recognition particle (a
CC       protein- and RNA-containing structure involved in endoplasmic-
CC       reticulum-associated protein synthesis).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.1.49.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00272;
//
ID   3.6.5.5
DE   Dynamin GTPase.
CA   GTP + H(2)O = GDP + phosphate.
CC   -!- An enzyme with a molecular mass of about 100 kDa that is involved in
CC       endocytosis and is instrumental in pinching off membrane vesicles.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.1.50.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00362;
