//
ID   4.1.1.99
DE   Phosphomevalonate decarboxylase.
CA   ATP + (R)-5-phosphomevalonate = ADP + phosphate + isopentenyl phosphate +
CA   CO(2).
CC   -!- The enzyme participates in a mevalonate pathway that occurs in
CC       archaea other than the extreme acidophiles of the Thermoplasmatales
CC       order.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 4.1.1.110.
DR   D4GXZ3, PMD_HALVD  ;
//
ID   4.1.1.100
DE   Prephenate decarboxylase.
AN   Non-aromatizing prephenate decarboxylase.
CA   Prephenate = 3-((4R)-4-hydroxycyclohexa-1,5-dien-1-yl)-2-oxopropanoate +
CA   CO(2).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Bacillus subtilis,
CC       is involved in the biosynthesis of the nonribosomally synthesized
CC       dipeptide antibiotic bacilysin, composed of L-alanine and
CC       L-anticapsin.
CC   -!- The enzyme isomerizes only the pro-R double bond in prephenate.
DR   A0A073CEA3, AERD_PLAAG ;  Q8KWT1, BACA_BACAM ;  Q8KWT6, BACA_BACIU ;
DR   P39638, BACA_BACSU ;
//
ID   4.1.1.101
DE   Malolactic enzyme.
CA   (S)-malate = (S)-lactate + CO(2).
CF   Mn(2+); NAD(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme is involved in the malolactic fermentation of wine, which
CC       results in a natural decrease in acidity and favorable changes in
CC       wine flavors.
CC   -!- It has been purified from several lactic acid bacteria, including
CC       Leuconostoc mesenteroides, Lactobacillus plantarum, and Oenococcus
CC       oeni.
DR   Q48662, MLES_LACLA ;  F9UMS6, MLES_LACPL ;  A0A095AMW7, MLES_LEUME ;
DR   Q48796, MLES_OENOE ;
//
ID   4.1.1.102
DE   Phenacrylate decarboxylase.
AN   Ferulic acid decarboxylase.
CA   (1) 4-coumarate = 4-vinylphenol + CO(2).
CA   (2) Trans-cinnamate = styrene + CO(2).
CA   (3) Ferulate = 4-vinylguaiacol + CO(2).
CF   Prenyl-FMNH(2).
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in fungi, catalyzes the decarboxylation of
CC       phenacrylic acids present in plant cell walls.
CC   -!- It requires a prenylated flavin cofactor that is produced by
CC       EC 2.5.1.129.
DR   A2QHE5, FDC1_ASPNC ;  B9WJ66, FDC1_CANDC ;  Q03034, FDC1_YEAST ;
DR   O07006, PADC_BACSU ;
//
ID   4.1.1.103
DE   Gamma-resorcylate decarboxylase.
CA   2,6-dihydroxybenzoate = 1,3-dihydroxybenzen + CO(2).
CF   Zn(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from several bacterial strains, is involved
CC       in the degradation of gamma-resorcylate.
CC   -!- The reaction is reversible, but equilibrium greatly favors the
CC       decarboxylation reaction.
//
ID   4.1.1.104
DE   3-dehydro-4-phosphotetronate decarboxylase.
CA   (1) 3-dehydro-4-phospho-D-erythronate = glycerone phosphate + CO(2).
CA   (2) 3-dehydro-4-phospho-L-erythronate = glycerone phosphate + CO(2).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from bacteria, is involved in D-erythronate
CC       and L-threonate catabolism.
DR   Q0KBC9, OTNC_CUPNH ;  A0A0H2VA12, OTNC_ECOL6 ;  Q46890, OTNC_ECOLI ;
DR   Q57199, OTNC_HAEIN ;  Q6CZ24, OTNC_PECAS ;
//
ID   4.1.1.105
DE   L-tryptophan decarboxylase.
CA   L-tryptophan = tryptamine + CO(2).
CC   -!- The enzyme has been characterized from bacteria, plants, and fungi.
CC   -!- Unlike EC 4.1.1.28 this enzyme is specific for L-typtophan.
DR   P0DPA6, PSID_PSICU ;  P93082, TDC1_CAMAC ;  P93083, TDC2_CAMAC ;
//
ID   4.1.1.106
DE   Fatty acid photodecarboxylase.
CA   A long-chain fatty acid + h-nu = a long-chain alkane + CO(2).
CC   -!- This algal enzyme, characterized from the green algae Chlorella
CC       variabilis and Chlamydomonas reinhardtii, is dependent on blue light,
CC       which photooxidizes its FAD cofactor.
CC   -!- The enzyme acts on fatty acids in the range of C(12) to C(22), with a
CC       higher efficiency for C(16) to C(17) chains, and forms an alkane
CC       product that is one carbon shorter than the substrate.
CC   -!- The enzyme can also act on unsaturated fatty acids, forming the
CC       respective alkenes, but does not generate a new double bond.
//
ID   4.1.1.107
DE   3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde synthase.
CA   L-dopa + O(2) + H(2)O = 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde + CO(2) + NH(3) +
CA   H(2)O(2).
CF   Pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the mosquito Aedes aegypti, catalyzes the
CC       production of 3,4-dihydroxylphenylacetaldehyde directly from L-dopa.
CC   -!- Dopamine is not formed as an intermediate (cf. EC 4.1.1.28).
CC   -!- The enzyme is specific for L-dopa and does not react with other
CC       aromatic amino acids with the exception of a low activity with alpha-
CC       methyl-L-dopa.
//
ID   4.1.1.108
DE   4-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde synthase.
CA   L-tyrosine + O(2) + H(2)O = (4-hydroxyphenyl)acetaldehyde + CO(2) + NH(3)
CA   + H(2)O(2).
CF   Pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the the plant Petroselinum crispum
CC       (parsley), catalyzes the production of 4-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde
CC       directly from L-tyrosine.
CC   -!- Tyramine is not formed as an intermediate.
CC   -!- The enzyme has a low activity with L-dopa (cf. EC 4.1.1.107).
//
ID   4.1.1.109
DE   Phenylacetaldehyde synthase.
CA   L-phenylalanine + O(2) + H(2)O = phenylacetaldehyde + CO(2) + NH(3) +
CA   H(2)O(2).
CF   Pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the the plants Petunia hybrida and a Rosa
CC       hybrid, catalyzes the production of phenylacetaldehyde directly from
CC       L-phenylalanine.
CC   -!- The enzyme is specific for L-phenylalanine and does not accept other
CC       aromatic amino acids as substrates.
//
ID   4.1.1.110
DE   Bisphosphomevalonate decarboxylase.
AN   Mevalonate 3,5-bisphosphate decarboxylase.
CA   (R)-3,5-bisphosphomevalonate = isopentenyl phosphate + CO(2) + phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme participates in an alternative mevalonate pathway that
CC       takes place in extreme acidophiles of the Thermoplasmatales order.
CC   -!- cf. EC 4.1.1.99, phosphomevalonate decarboxylase.
DR   Q6L1T9, MBD_PICTO  ;  Q9HJS1, MBD_THEAC  ;  Q97BM8, MBD_THEVO  ;
//
ID   4.1.1.111
DE   Siroheme decarboxylase.
CA   Siroheme = 12,18-didecarboxysiroheme + 2 CO(2).
CC   -!- The enzyme from archaea is involved in an alternative heme
CC       biosynthesis pathway.
CC   -!- The enzyme from denitrifying bacteria is involved in the heme d1
CC       biosynthesis pathway.
//
ID   4.1.1.112
DE   Oxaloacetate decarboxylase.
AN   Oxalate beta-decarboxylase.
AN   Oxaloacetate carboxy-lyase.
CA   Oxaloacetate = pyruvate + CO(2).
CC   -!- The enzymes from the fish Gadus morhua (Atlantic cod) and the
CC       bacterium Micrococcus luteus prefer Mn(2+), while those from the
CC       bacteria Pseudomonas putida and Pseudomonas aeruginosa prefer Mg(2+).
CC   -!- Unlike EC 7.2.4.2, there is no evidence of the enzyme's involvement
CC       in Na(+) transport.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.1.1.3.
PR   PROSITE; PDOC00167;
PR   PROSITE; PDOC50991;
