ID   4.1.3.41
DE   3-hydroxy-D-aspartate aldolase.
AN   D-3-hydroxyaspartate aldolase.
CA   (1) Threo-3-hydroxy-D-aspartate = glycine + glyoxylate.
CA   (2) D-erythro-3-hydroxyaspartate = glycine + glyoxylate.
CF   Pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, purified from the bacterium Paracoccus denitrificansy IFO
CC       13301, is strictly D-specific as to the alpha-position of the
CC       substrate, but accepts both the threo and erythro forms at the beta-
CC       position.
CC   -!- The erythro form is a far better substrate (about 100 fold).
CC   -!- The enzyme can also accept D-allothreonine, D-threonine, erythro-3-
CC       phenyl-D-serine and threo-3-phenyl-D-serine.
CC   -!- Different from EC 4.1.3.14.
CC   -!- Requires a divalent cation, such as Mg(2+), Mn(2+) or Co(2+).
DR   Q8GRC8, DHAA_PARDE ;
//
ID   4.1.3.42
DE   (4S)-4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate aldolase.
AN   2-keto-4-hydroxyglutaric aldolase.
AN   4-hydroxy-2-ketoglutaric aldolase.
AN   Hydroxyketoglutaric aldolase.
CA   (4S)-4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate = pyruvate + glyoxylate.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Escherichia coli is specific for the
CC       (S) enantiomer.
CC   -!- That enzyme is trifunctional, and also catalyzes the reactions of
CC       EC 4.1.1.3 and EC 4.1.2.14.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 4.1.3.16.
//
ID   4.1.3.43
DE   4-hydroxy-2-oxohexanoate aldolase.
CA   (S)-4-hydroxy-2-oxohexanoate = propanal + pyruvate.
CF   Mn(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacteria Burkholderia xenovorans and Thermus
CC       thermophilus also perform the reaction of EC 4.1.3.39.
CC   -!- The enzymes form a bifunctional complex with EC 1.2.1.89 with a tight
CC       channel connecting the two subunits.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.1.3.n1.
DR   P51015, HOA4_PARXL ;  Q53WI0, HOA_THET8  ;
//
ID   4.1.3.44
DE   tRNA 4-demethylwyosine synthase (AdoMet-dependent).
CA   N(1)-methylguanine(37) in tRNA(Phe) + pyruvate + S-adenosyl-L-methionine
CA   = 4-demethylwyosine(37) in tRNA(Phe) + L-methionine + 5'-deoxyadenosine +
CA   CO(2) + H(2)O.
CF   Iron-sulfur.
CC   -!- This enzyme, which is a member of the superfamily of S-adenosyl-L-
CC       methionine-dependent radical (radical AdoMet) enzymes, binds two
CC       [4Fe-4S] clusters.
CC   -!- Carbons C(2) and C(3) from pyruvate are incorporated into
CC       4-demethylwyosine.
CC   -!- The enzyme is found in eukaryotes where it is part of the pathway for
CC       wybutosine synthesis, and in archaea, where it is involved in the
CC       biosynthesis of archaeal wye bases, such as wyosine, isowyosine,
CC       and methylwyosine.
