ID   4.4.1.23
DE   2-hydroxypropyl-CoM lyase.
AN   Coenzyme M-epoxyalkane ligase.
AN   EaCoMT.
CA   (1) (R)-2-hydroxypropyl-CoM = (R)-1,2-epoxypropane + HS-CoM.
CA   (2) (S)-2-hydroxypropyl-CoM = (S)-1,2-epoxypropane + HS-CoM.
CF   Zn(2+).
CC   -!- Acts on both enantiomers of chiral epoxyalkanes to form the
CC       corresponding (R)- and (S)-2-hydroxyalkyl-CoM adducts.
CC   -!- The enzyme will function with some other thiols (e.g.,
CC       2-sulfanylethanol) as the nucleophile.
CC   -!- Uses short-chain epoxyalkanes from C(2) (epoxyethane) to C(6) (1,2-
CC       epoxyhexane).
CC   -!- This enzyme forms component I of a four-component enzyme system
CC       (comprising EC 4.4.1.23 (component I), EC 1.8.1.5 (component II),
CC       EC 1.1.1.268 (component III) and EC 1.1.1.269 (component IV)) that is
CC       involved in epoxyalkane carboxylation in Xanthobacter sp. strain Py2.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.2.99.19.
DR   Q56837, XECA_XANP2 ;
//
ID   4.4.1.24
DE   (2R)-sulfolactate sulfo-lyase.
AN   Sulfolactate sulfo-lyase.
CA   (2R)-3-sulfolactate = pyruvate + hydrogensulfite.
CF   Fe(2+).
CC   -!- This inducible enzyme participates in cysteate degradation in
CC       Paracoccus pantotrophus NKNCYSA and in 3-sulfolactate degradation in
CC       Chromohalobacter salexigens.
CC   -!- The enzyme is specific for the (R) isomer.
DR   Q1QWP1, SUYA_CHRSD ;  Q58Y44, SUYA_PARPN ;  Q1QWP0, SUYB_CHRSD ;
DR   Q58Y43, SUYB_PARPN ;
//
ID   4.4.1.25
DE   L-cysteate sulfo-lyase.
CA   L-cysteate + H(2)O = hydrogensulfite + pyruvate + NH(3).
CF   Pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme cleaves a carbon-sulfur bond, releasing bisulfite and an
CC       unstable enamine product that tautomerizes to an imine form, which
CC       undergoes a hydrolytic deamination to form pyruvate and ammonia.
CC   -!- The latter reaction, which can occur spontaneously, can also be
CC       catalyzed by EC 3.5.99.10.
CC   -!- D-cysteine can also act as a substrate, but more slowly.
CC   -!- It is converted into pyruvate, sulfide and ammonia.
CC   -!- The inducible enzyme from Silicibacter pomeroyi DSS-3 forms part of
CC       the cysteate-degradation pathway.
DR   Q5LL69, CUYA_RUEPO ;
//
ID   4.4.1.26
DE   Olivetolic acid cyclase.
CA   3,5,7-trioxododecanoyl-CoA = CoA + 2,4-dihydroxy-6-pentylbenzoate.
CC   -!- Part of the cannabinoids biosynthetic pathway in the plant Cannabis
CC       sativa.
DR   I6WU39, OLIAC_CANSA;
//
ID   4.4.1.27
DE   Transferred entry: 3.13.1.5.
//
ID   4.4.1.28
DE   L-cysteine desulfidase.
AN   L-cysteine desulfhydrase.
CA   L-cysteine + H(2)O = sulfide + NH(3) + pyruvate.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the archaeon Methanocaldococcus jannaschii contains a
CC       [4Fe-4S] cluster and is specific for L-cysteine (cf. EC 4.4.1.1).
CC   -!- It cleaves a carbon-sulfur bond releasing sulfide and the unstable
CC       enamine product 2-aminoprop-2-enoate that tautomerizes to an imine
CC       form, which undergoes a hydrolytic deamination to form pyruvate and
CC       ammonia.
CC   -!- The same reaction can also be catalyzed by some pyridoxal-phosphate
CC       proteins (cf. EC 4.4.1.1).
DR   Q58431, CYDE_METJA ;  A4FW56, CYDE_METM5 ;  A9A9J5, CYDE_METM6 ;
DR   A6VH54, CYDE_METM7 ;  Q6LX84, CYDE_METMP ;  Q9M1R1, LCYD1_ARATH;
DR   Q9LGZ2, LCYD1_ORYSJ;  Q5JNT6, LCYD2_ORYSJ;  Q8RDT4, MEGL_FUSNN ;
DR   P06721, METC_ECOLI ;
//
ID   4.4.1.29
DE   Phycobiliprotein cysteine-84 phycobilin lyase.
CA   (1) [C-phycocyanin beta-subunit]-Cys(84)-phycocyanobilin = apo-
CA   [C-phycocyanin beta-subunit] + (2R,3E)-phycocyanobilin.
CA   (2) [Phycoerythrocyanin beta-subunit]-Cys(84)-phycocyanobilin = apo-
CA   [phycoerythrocyanin beta-subunit] + (2R,3E)-phycocyanobilin.
CA   (3) [Allophycocyanin alpha-subunit]-Cys(84)-phycocyanobilin = apo-
