ID   4.4.1.35
DE   L-cystine beta-lyase.
CA   L-cystine + H(2)O = L-thiocysteine + pyruvate + NH(3).
CF   Pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme cleaves a carbon-sulfur bond, releasing L-thiocysteine and
CC       an unstable enamine product that tautomerizes to an imine form, which
CC       undergoes a hydrolytic deamination to form pyruvate and ammonia.
CC   -!- The latter reaction, which can occur spontaneously, can also be
CC       catalyzed by EC 3.5.99.10.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Brassica oleracea var. italica (broccoli) does not
CC       act on cysteine or cystathionine.
DR   Q9SUR6, CORI3_ARATH;
//
ID   4.4.1.36
DE   Hercynylcysteine S-oxide lyase.
CA   S-(hercyn-2-yl)-L-cysteine S-oxide + a reduced electron acceptor =
CA   ergothioneine + pyruvate + ammonia + an oxidized electron acceptor.
CF   Pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Mycobacterium smegmatis,
CC       cayalyses the last step in the pathway of ergothioneine biosynthesis.
CC   -!- The enzyme forms a 2-sulfenohercynine intermediate, which is reduced
CC       to ergothioneine non-enzymically by a thiol.
CC   -!- In vitro, DTT can serve this function.
//
ID   4.4.1.37
DE   Pyridinium-3,5-bisthiocarboxylic acid mononucleotide synthase.
AN   P2CMN sulfurtransferase.
AN   P2TMN synthase.
AN   Pyridinium-3,5-biscarboxylic acid mononucleotide sulfurtransferase.
CA   (1) [LarE]-L-cysteine + pyridinium-3,5-dicarboxylate mononucleotide +
CA   ATP = [LarE]-dehydroalanine + pyridinium-3-carboxylate-5-thiocarboxylate
CA   mononucleotide + AMP + diphosphate.
CA   (2) [LarE]-L-cysteine + pyridinium-3-carboxylate-5-thiocarboxylate
CA   mononucleotide + ATP = [LarE]-dehydroalanine + pyridinium-3,5-
CA   bisthiocarboxylate mononucleotide + AMP + diphosphate.
CC   -!- This enzyme, found in Lactobacillus plantarum, is involved in the
CC       biosynthesis of a nickel-pincer cofactor.
CC   -!- The process starts when one enzyme molecule adenylates pyridinium-
CC       3,5-dicarboxylate mononucleotide (P2CMN) and covalently binds the
CC       adenylated product to an intrinsic cysteine residue.
CC   -!- Next, the enzyme cleaves the carbon-sulfur bond, liberating
CC       pyridinium-3-carboxylate-5-thiocarboxylate mononucleotide (PCTMN) and
CC       leaving a 2-aminoprop-2-enoate (dehydroalanine) residue attached to
CC       the protein.
CC   -!- Since the cysteine residue is not regenerated in vivo, the enzyme is
CC       inactivated during the process.
CC   -!- A second enzyme molecule then repeats the process with PCTMN,
CC       adenylating it and covalently binding it to the same cysteine
CC       residue, followed by liberation of pyridinium-3,5-bisthiocarboxylate
CC       mononucleotide (P2TMN) and the inactivation of the second enzyme
CC       molecule.
