//
ID   6.3.5.10
DE   Adenosylcobyric acid synthase (glutamine-hydrolyzing).
AN   5'-deoxy-5'-adenosylcobyrinic-acid-a,c-diamide:L-glutamine amido-ligase.
AN   Ado-cobyric acid synthase (glutamine hydrolyzing).
AN   Cobyric acid synthase.
CA   4 ATP + adenosylcobyrinic acid a,c-diamide + 4 L-glutamine + 4 H(2)O =
CA   4 ADP + 4 phosphate + adenosylcobyric acid + 4 L-glutamate.
CF   Mg(2+).
CC   -!- NH(3) can act instead of glutamine.
CC   -!- This enzyme catalyzes the four-step amidation sequence from cobyrinic
CC       acid a,c-diamide to cobyric acid via the formation of cobyrinic acid
CC       triamide, tetraamide and pentaamide intermediates.
//
ID   6.3.5.11
DE   Cobyrinate a,c-diamide synthase (glutamine-hydrolyzing).
AN   Cobyrinic acid a,c-diamide synthetase.
CA   2 ATP + cobyrinate + 2 L-glutamine + 2 H(2)O = 2 ADP + 2 phosphate +
CA   cobyrinate a,c-diamide + 2 L-glutamate.
CC   -!- This enzyme is the first glutamine amidotransferase that participates
CC       in the anaerobic (early cobalt insertion) biosynthetic pathway of
CC       adenosylcobalamin, and catalyzes the ATP-dependent synthesis of
CC       cobyrinate a,c-diamide from cobyrinate using either L-glutamine or
CC       ammonia as the nitrogen source.
CC   -!- It is proposed that the enzyme first catalyzes the amidation of the
CC       c-carboxylate, and then the intermediate is released into solution
CC       and binds to the same catalytic site for the amidation of the
CC       a-carboxylate.
CC   -!- The Km for ammonia is substantially higher than that for L-glutamine.
