ID   7.2.2.4
DE   ABC-type Na(+) transporter.
CA   ATP + H(2)O + Na(+)(Side 1) = ADP + phosphate + Na(+)(Side 2).
CC   -!- ABC-type (ATP-binding cassette-type) transporter, characterized by
CC       the presence of two similar ATP-binding domains/proteins and two
CC       integral membrane domains/proteins.
CC   -!- This bacterial enzyme, characterized from Bacillus subtilis, exports
CC       Na(+) ions out of the cell.
CC   -!- Cf. EC 7.2.2.1 and EC 7.2.2.3.
//
ID   7.2.4.1
DE   Carboxybiotin decarboxylase.
AN   Carboxybiotin protein decarboxylase.
CA   A carboxybiotinyl-[protein] + 1-2 Na(+)(In) + H(+)(Out) = CO(2) +
CA   a biotinyl-[protein] + 1-2 Na(+)(Out).
CC   -!- The integral membrane protein MadB from the anaerobic bacterium
CC       Malonomonas rubra is a component of the multienzyme complex
CC       EC 4.1.1.89.
CC   -!- The free energy of the decarboxylation reaction is used to pump Na(+)
CC       out of the cell.
CC   -!- The enzyme is a member of the Na(+)-translocating decarboxylase
CC       family, other members of which include EC 7.2.4.2 and EC 7.2.4.3.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.3.99.2.
DR   O06923, MADB_MALRU ;
//
ID   7.2.4.2
DE   Oxaloacetate decarboxylase (Na(+) extruding).
AN   Oxalacetic acid decarboxylase.
AN   Oxalate beta-decarboxylase.
AN   Oxaloacetate beta-decarboxylase.
CA   Oxaloacetate + 2 Na(+)(Side 1) = pyruvate + CO(2) + 2 Na(+)(Side 2).
CC   -!- The enzyme from Klebsiella aerogenes is a biotinyl protein and also
CC       decarboxylates glutaconyl-CoA and methylmalonyl-CoA.
CC   -!- The process is accompanied by the extrusion of two sodium ions from
CC       cells.
CC   -!- Some animal enzymes require Mn(2+).
CC   -!- Differs from EC 4.1.1.112 (oxaloacetate decarboxylase) for which
CC       there is no evidence for involvement in Na(+) transport.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.1.1.3.
