ID   7.2.4.3
DE   (S)-methylmalonyl-CoA decarboxylase (sodium-transporting).
AN   (S)-2-methyl-3-oxopropanoyl-CoA carboxy-lyase.
AN   (S)-methylmalonyl-CoA carboxy-lyase.
AN   Methylmalonyl-coenzyme A decarboxylase.
CA   (S)-methylmalonyl-CoA + Na(+)(Side 1) + H(+)(Side 2) = propanoyl-CoA +
CA   CO(2) + Na(+)(Side 2).
CF   Biotin.
CC   -!- This bacterial enzyme couples the decarboxylation of (S)-
CC       methylmalonyl-CoA to propanoyl-CoA to the vectorial transport of
CC       Na(+) across the cytoplasmic membrane, thereby creating a sodium ion
CC       motive force that is used for ATP synthesis.
CC   -!- It is a membrane-associated biotin protein and is strictly dependent
CC       on sodium ions for activity.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.1.1.41.
DR   Q2HJD5, ECHD1_BOVIN;  F1NB38, ECHD1_CHICK;  F1R6N4, ECHD1_DANRE;
DR   Q9NTX5, ECHD1_HUMAN;  Q9D9V3, ECHD1_MOUSE;  Q5R4W0, ECHD1_PONAB;
DR   Q6AYG5, ECHD1_RAT  ;  Q5HZQ8, ECHD1_XENLA;  Q28C91, ECHD1_XENTR;
//
ID   7.3.2.1
DE   ABC-type phosphate transporter.
AN   ABC phosphate transporter.
AN   Phosphate-transporting ATPase.
CA   ATP + H(2)O + phosphate-[phosphate-binding protein](Side 1) = ADP +
CA   phosphate + phosphate(Side 2) + [phosphate-binding protein](Side 1).
CC   -!- An ATP-binding cassette (ABC) type transporter, characterized by the
CC       presence of two similar ATP-binding domains/proteins and two integral
CC       membrane domains/proteins.
CC   -!- A bacterial enzyme that interacts with an extracytoplasmic substrate
CC       binding protein and mediates the high affinity uptake of phosphate
CC       anions.
CC   -!- Unlike P-type ATPases, it does not undergo phosphorylation during the
CC       transport process.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.3.27.
